<div class="ltx_pagination ltx_role_newpage"></div>
<section id="Ax1" class="ltx_appendix">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix">Appendix</h2>

<div id="Ax1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="Ax1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In this Appendix we provide supplemental content including proofs of the theoretical results, optimization steps for <span id="Ax1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_markedasmath">AODL</span> and baselines, as well as <span id="Ax1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_markedasmath">AODL</span> in the presence of missing values, datasets description, additional evaluation results, and hyperparameter tuning protocol to aid reproducibility.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A1" class="ltx_appendix">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_appendix">Appendix A </span>Proof of Theorem <a href="#Thmtheorem1" title="Theorem 1 (Generalization bound for two-way dictionary learning). ‣ 4 Problem formulation and sample complexity ‣ Sparse Coding Representation of 2-way Data" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a></h2>

<div id="A1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In this section, we prove Theorem <a href="#Thmtheorem1" title="Theorem 1 (Generalization bound for two-way dictionary learning). ‣ 4 Problem formulation and sample complexity ‣ Sparse Coding Representation of 2-way Data" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>, which gives a generalization bound for two-way dictionary learning.
Our sample complexity analysis generalizes the one in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib42" title="" class="ltx_ref">42</a>]</cite> (see also <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib18" title="" class="ltx_ref">18</a>]</cite>), which is for the case of a single dictionary, with real-valued vector signals. Our generalization proceeds by viewing our signal matrices as vectors in an appropriate space and dictionary pairs as linear operators on that space.</p>
</div>
<div id="A1.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">To derive a sample complexity bound, we will derive what is known as a <em id="A1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">uniform convergence bound</em>. This is a high-probability bound on
the <em id="A1.p2.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">generalization gap</em> function, defined as follows:</p>
<table id="A1.EGx8" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="A1.E12"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A1.E12.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\Psi(\mathcal{X}):=\sup_{h\in\mathcal{H}}|R(h)-\hat{R}(h,\mathcal{X})|." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Ψ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.1389em">:=</mo><mrow><munder><mo lspace="0.1389em" movablelimits="false" rspace="0em">sup</mo><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi></mrow></munder><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>h</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>R</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>h</mi><mo>,</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\Psi(\mathcal{X}):=\sup_{h\in\mathcal{H}}|R(h)-\hat{R}(h,\mathcal{X})|.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(12)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A1.p2.2" class="ltx_p">The bound must hold regardless of the data-generating distribution <math id="A1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{D}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{D}</annotation></semantics></math>. Such a bound constitutes a <em id="A1.p2.2.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">generalization bound</em> for empirical risk minimization, which yields a sample complexity bound.</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmdefinition1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_definition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmdefinition1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Definition 1</span></span><span id="Thmdefinition1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Covering numbers of a metric space)<span id="Thmdefinition1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmdefinition1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="Thmdefinition1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmdefinition1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Consider a metric space <math id="Thmdefinition1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math> with metric <math id="Thmdefinition1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math> and a real number <math id="Thmdefinition1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>γ</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math>. We say that a collection <math id="Thmdefinition1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S\subseteq M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo>⊆</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S\subseteq M</annotation></semantics></math> is a <math id="Thmdefinition1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>γ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma</annotation></semantics></math>-covering of <math id="Thmdefinition1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math> if, for all <math id="Thmdefinition1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x\in M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x\in M</annotation></semantics></math>, there exists <math id="Thmdefinition1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="z\in S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>z</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">z\in S</annotation></semantics></math> such that <math id="Thmdefinition1.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d(x,z)\leq\gamma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>z</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mi>γ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d(x,z)\leq\gamma</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="Thmdefinition1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmdefinition1.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmdefinition1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">The <math id="Thmdefinition1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>γ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma</annotation></semantics></math>-covering number of <math id="Thmdefinition1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math>, denoted by <math id="Thmdefinition1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{N}(M,\gamma)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>γ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{N}(M,\gamma)</annotation></semantics></math>, is given by the cardinality of the minimum-size
<math id="Thmdefinition1.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>γ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma</annotation></semantics></math>-covering of <math id="Thmdefinition1.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="A1.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">For the purpose of deriving a generalization bound, we must bound the covering numbers of the following <em id="A1.p3.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">loss class</em>, which is
a class of functions <math id="A1.p3.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\mathcal{F}:=\{f_{h}:\Omega\to\mathbb{R}~|~h\in\mathcal{H}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>h</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>ℝ</mi><mo fence="false" lspace="0.330em" rspace="0.497em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>h</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{F}:=\{f_{h}:\Omega\to\mathbb{R}~|~h\in\mathcal{H}\}</annotation></semantics></math> induced by composing hypotheses in <math id="A1.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{H}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{H}</annotation></semantics></math> with the loss function.</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmdefinition2" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_definition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmdefinition2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Definition 2</span></span><span id="Thmdefinition2.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Loss class associated with a hypothesis class)<span id="Thmdefinition2.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmdefinition2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmdefinition2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmdefinition2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">For a fixed loss function <math id="Thmdefinition2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ell:\mathcal{H}\times\Omega\to\mathbb{R}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>ℝ</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ell:\mathcal{H}\times\Omega\to\mathbb{R}</annotation></semantics></math> and a hypothesis class <math id="Thmdefinition2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{H}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{H}</annotation></semantics></math>, the
<em id="Thmdefinition2.p1.1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_upright">loss class</em> <math id="Thmdefinition2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{F}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{F}</annotation></semantics></math> induced by <math id="Thmdefinition2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{H}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{H}</annotation></semantics></math> is given by</span></p>
<table id="A1.EGx9" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="A1.E13"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A1.E13.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\mathcal{F}:=\{f_{h}(x):=\ell(h,x)~|~h\in\mathcal{H}\}." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>h</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>h</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>x</mi><mo rspace="0.052em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.330em">|</mo><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mathcal{F}:=\{f_{h}(x):=\ell(h,x)~|~h\in\mathcal{H}\}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(13)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
<div id="A1.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">The relevant metric on <math id="A1.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{F}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{F}</annotation></semantics></math> is the one induced by the <math id="A1.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L_{\infty}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>L</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L_{\infty}</annotation></semantics></math> norm: for <math id="A1.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f\in\mathcal{F}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f\in\mathcal{F}</annotation></semantics></math>,</p>
<table id="A1.EGx10" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="A1.E14"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A1.E14.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\|f\|_{\infty}:=\sup_{x\in\Omega}|f(x)|." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></msub><mo rspace="0.1389em">:=</mo><mrow><munder><mo lspace="0.1389em" movablelimits="false" rspace="0em">sup</mo><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi></mrow></munder><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\|f\|_{\infty}:=\sup_{x\in\Omega}|f(x)|.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(14)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="A1.p5" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A1.p5.1" class="ltx_p">We next give a standard generalization bound based on the <math id="A1.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L_{\infty}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>L</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L_{\infty}</annotation></semantics></math> covering numbers of a function class <math id="A1.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{F}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{F}</annotation></semantics></math>. This can be found
in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib42" title="" class="ltx_ref">42</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmlemma1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_lemma">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmlemma1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Lemma 1</span></span><span id="Thmlemma1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Generalization bound based on covering numbers of the loss class <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib42" title="" class="ltx_ref">42</a>]</cite>)<span id="Thmlemma1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmlemma1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmlemma1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmlemma1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="Thmlemma1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{F}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{F}</annotation></semantics></math> denote a class of functions satisfying, for all <math id="Thmlemma1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>f</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="Thmlemma1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f(x)\in[0,B]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>B</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f(x)\in[0,B]</annotation></semantics></math>. Then for all <math id="Thmlemma1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math>,
we have that with probability at least <math id="Thmlemma1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1-e^{-x}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1-e^{-x}</annotation></semantics></math> over <math id="Thmlemma1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>m</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m</annotation></semantics></math> samples <math id="Thmlemma1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{1},...,X_{m}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>m</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{1},...,X_{m}</annotation></semantics></math> sampled iid from an arbitrary
distribution <math id="Thmlemma1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{D}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{D}</annotation></semantics></math>, we have, for all <math id="Thmlemma1.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f\in\mathcal{F}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f\in\mathcal{F}</annotation></semantics></math>,</span></p>
<table id="A1.EGx11" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="A1.E15"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A1.E15.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle|\mathbb{E}_{X\sim\mathcal{D}}[f(X)]-\frac{1}{m}\sum_{j=1}^{m}f(X_{j})|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>∼</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>m</mi></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>m</mi></munderover></mstyle><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle|\mathbb{E}_{X\sim\mathcal{D}}[f(X)]-\frac{1}{m}\sum_{j=1}^{m}f(X_{j})|</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(15)</span></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="A1.E16"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A1.E16.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\leq 2\epsilon+B\cdot\left(\sqrt{\frac{\log\mathcal{N}(\mathcal{F},L_{\infty},\epsilon)}{2m}}+\sqrt{\frac{x}{2m}}\right)." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ϵ</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><msqrt><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>L</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>ϵ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow></mfrac></mstyle></msqrt><mo>+</mo><msqrt><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mi>x</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow></mfrac></mstyle></msqrt></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\leq 2\epsilon+B\cdot\left(\sqrt{\frac{\log\mathcal{N}(\mathcal{F},L_{\infty},\epsilon)}{2m}}+\sqrt{\frac{x}{2m}}\right).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(16)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
<div id="A1.p6" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A1.p6.1" class="ltx_p">The <math id="A1.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L_{\infty}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>L</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L_{\infty}</annotation></semantics></math> covering number of <math id="A1.p6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{F}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{F}</annotation></semantics></math> can be upper bounded using the covering numbers of <math id="A1.p6.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{H}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{H}</annotation></semantics></math> along with a bound on the Lipschitz
constant of <math id="A1.p6.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{h}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>h</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{h}</annotation></semantics></math> as a function of <math id="A1.p6.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>h</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h</annotation></semantics></math>. In order to make this precise, we must specify a norm on <math id="A1.p6.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{H}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{H}</annotation></semantics></math>. Recall the normalization
conditions on the outer products of columns of <math id="A1.p6.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>L</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L</annotation></semantics></math> with rows of <math id="A1.p6.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R^{T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>R</mi><mi>T</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R^{T}</annotation></semantics></math>. These translate to an operator norm condition on the mappings induced by pairs <math id="A1.p6.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(L,R)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>L</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>R</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(L,R)</annotation></semantics></math>. We have the following
standard definition.</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmdefinition3" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_definition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmdefinition3.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Definition 3</span></span><span id="Thmdefinition3.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Operator norms of a linear mapping)<span id="Thmdefinition3.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmdefinition3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmdefinition3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmdefinition3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="Thmdefinition3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V,W" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>W</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V,W</annotation></semantics></math> be normed spaces with norms <math id="Thmdefinition3.p1.m2" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\|\cdot\|_{V},\|\cdot\|_{W}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo rspace="0em">∥</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∥</mo><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>V</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∥</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo lspace="0em">∥</mo><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>W</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\|\cdot\|_{V},\|\cdot\|_{W}</annotation></semantics></math>, and let <math id="Thmdefinition3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F:V\to W" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>W</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F:V\to W</annotation></semantics></math> be a linear operator.
The operator norm <math id="Thmdefinition3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\|F\|_{op,V,W}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mi>F</mi><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>V</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>W</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\|F\|_{op,V,W}</annotation></semantics></math> is defined to be</span></p>
<table id="A1.EGx12" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="A1.E17"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A1.E17.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\sup_{x\in V}\frac{\|Fx\|_{W}}{\|x\|_{W}}." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false">sup</mo><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>V</mi></mrow></munder><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mi>W</mi></msub><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mi>W</mi></msub></mfrac></mstyle></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\sup_{x\in V}\frac{\|Fx\|_{W}}{\|x\|_{W}}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(17)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
<div id="A1.p7" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A1.p7.1" class="ltx_p">It is well known that the <math id="A1.p7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L_{1}\to L_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L_{1}\to L_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> operator norm of a matrix is equal to the maximum <math id="A1.p7.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> norm of any of its columns.
We thus will use the following norm for <math id="A1.p7.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{H}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{H}</annotation></semantics></math>: for any <math id="A1.p7.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(L,R)\in\mathcal{H}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>L</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>R</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(L,R)\in\mathcal{H}</annotation></semantics></math>,</p>
<table id="A1.EGx13" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="A1.E18"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A1.E18.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\|(L,R)\|_{op,1,2}:=\max_{Z}\frac{\|LZR^{T}\|_{F}}{\|Z\|_{L_{1}}}." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>L</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>R</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msub><mo>:=</mo><mrow><munder><mi>max</mi><mi>Z</mi></munder><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>Z</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>R</mi><mi>T</mi></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mi>F</mi></msub><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mi>Z</mi><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></msub></mfrac></mstyle></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\|(L,R)\|_{op,1,2}:=\max_{Z}\frac{\|LZR^{T}\|_{F}}{\|Z\|_{L_{1}}}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(18)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A1.p7.2" class="ltx_p">The advantage of this norm is that it turns <math id="A1.p7.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{H}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{H}</annotation></semantics></math> into a unit ball in a finite-dimensional Banach space, for which covering number bounds are known. Specifically, we have the following lemma.</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmlemma2" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_lemma">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmlemma2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Lemma 2</span></span><span id="Thmlemma2.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Covering numbers of Banach space balls (Proposition 5 of <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">8</a>]</cite>))<span id="Thmlemma2.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmlemma2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="Thmlemma2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmlemma2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="Thmlemma2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{B}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝔹</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{B}</annotation></semantics></math> denote a Banach space with norm <math id="Thmlemma2.p1.m2" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\|\cdot\|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo rspace="0em">∥</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo lspace="0em">∥</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\|\cdot\|</annotation></semantics></math>, with dimension <math id="Thmlemma2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\dim(\mathbb{B})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo rspace="0em">dim</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝔹</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\dim(\mathbb{B})</annotation></semantics></math>.
Then the <math id="Thmlemma2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\epsilon" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ϵ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\epsilon</annotation></semantics></math>-covering number of a ball <math id="Thmlemma2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B(0,R)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>R</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B(0,R)</annotation></semantics></math> with radius <math id="Thmlemma2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>R</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R</annotation></semantics></math> is upper bounded as follows:</span></p>
<table id="A1.EGx14" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="A1.E19"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A1.E19.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\log\mathcal{N}(B(0,R),\epsilon)\leq\dim(\mathbb{B})\log\left(\frac{4R}{\epsilon}\right)." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>R</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>ϵ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.1389em">≤</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0.1389em" rspace="0em">dim</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝔹</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>R</mi></mrow><mi>ϵ</mi></mfrac></mstyle><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\log\mathcal{N}(B(0,R),\epsilon)\leq\dim(\mathbb{B})\log\left(\frac{4R}{\epsilon}\right).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(19)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="Thmlemma2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmlemma2.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmlemma2.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Since the dimension of <math id="Thmlemma2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{H}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{H}</annotation></semantics></math> is given by <math id="Thmlemma2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="NP+MQ" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>P</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>Q</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">NP+MQ</annotation></semantics></math>, this results in the bound</span></p>
<table id="A1.EGx15" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="A1.E20"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A1.E20.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\log\mathcal{N}(\mathcal{H},\epsilon)\leq(NP+MQ)\log\left(\frac{4}{\epsilon}\right)." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ϵ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>P</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>Q</mi></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mn>4</mn><mi>ϵ</mi></mfrac></mstyle><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\log\mathcal{N}(\mathcal{H},\epsilon)\leq(NP+MQ)\log\left(\frac{4}{\epsilon}\right).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(20)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
<div id="A1.p8" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A1.p8.1" class="ltx_p">We next turn to translating the covering number bounds for <math id="A1.p8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{H}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{H}</annotation></semantics></math> to bounds for <math id="A1.p8.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{F}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{F}</annotation></semantics></math>. The following well known lemma is the vehicle for this translation.</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmlemma3" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_lemma">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmlemma3.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Lemma 3</span></span><span id="Thmlemma3.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Lipschitz bound for covering numbers)<span id="Thmlemma3.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmlemma3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmlemma3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmlemma3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="Thmlemma3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M_{1},M_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>M</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>M</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M_{1},M_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> be two metric spaces. Suppose that <math id="Thmlemma3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f:M_{1}\to M_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msub><mi>M</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mi>M</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f:M_{1}\to M_{2}</annotation></semantics></math>
is a <math id="Thmlemma3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>λ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda</annotation></semantics></math>-Lipschitz function. Then</span></p>
<table id="A1.EGx16" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="A1.E21"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A1.E21.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\mathcal{N}(f(M_{1}),\epsilon)\leq\mathcal{N}(M_{1},\epsilon/\lambda)." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>M</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>ϵ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>M</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>ϵ</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>λ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mathcal{N}(f(M_{1}),\epsilon)\leq\mathcal{N}(M_{1},\epsilon/\lambda).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(21)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
<div id="A1.p9" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A1.p9.1" class="ltx_p">In the next lemma, we bound the Lipschitz constant for the map <math id="A1.p9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G:\mathcal{H}\to\mathcal{F}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G:\mathcal{H}\to\mathcal{F}</annotation></semantics></math> defined by
<math id="A1.p9.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G(h):=f_{h}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>h</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>:=</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>h</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G(h):=f_{h}</annotation></semantics></math>. We recall that the norm on <math id="A1.p9.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{F}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{F}</annotation></semantics></math> is the <math id="A1.p9.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L_{\infty}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>L</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L_{\infty}</annotation></semantics></math> norm.</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmlemma4" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_lemma">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmlemma4.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Lemma 4</span></span><span id="Thmlemma4.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Lipschitz constant bound for the hypothesis to loss function mapping)<span id="Thmlemma4.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmlemma4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmlemma4.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmlemma4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">The mapping <math id="Thmlemma4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> is such that, for all <math id="Thmlemma4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h,h^{\prime}\in\mathcal{H}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>h</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h,h^{\prime}\in\mathcal{H}</annotation></semantics></math>,</span></p>
<table id="A1.EGx17" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="A1.E22"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A1.E22.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\|G(h)-G(h^{\prime})\|_{\infty}\leq 2\cdot\kappa\|h-h^{\prime}\|_{op,1,2}." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>h</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>h</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></msub><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mi>κ</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>−</mo><msup><mi>h</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\|G(h)-G(h^{\prime})\|_{\infty}\leq 2\cdot\kappa\|h-h^{\prime}\|_{op,1,2}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(22)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
<div id="A1.2" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="A1.p10" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.p10.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.p10.1.1" class="ltx_text">We expand out the definition of <math id="A1.p10.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> and the <math id="A1.p10.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L_{\infty}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>L</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L_{\infty}</annotation></semantics></math> norm:</span></p>
<table id="A1.EGx18" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="A1.E23"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A1.E23.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\|G(L,R)-G(L^{\prime},R^{\prime})\|_{\infty}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>L</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>R</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>L</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>R</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\|G(L,R)-G(L^{\prime},R^{\prime})\|_{\infty}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(23)</span></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="A1.E24"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A1.E24.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\max_{X}|\min_{Y,W}\|X-LYWR^{T}\|_{F}^{2}-\min_{Y^{\prime},W^{\prime}}\|X-L^{\prime}Y^{\prime}W^{\prime}R^{\prime T}\|_{F}^{2}|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><munder><mi>max</mi><mi>X</mi></munder><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><mrow><munder><mi>min</mi><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>W</mi></mrow></munder><mo>⁡</mo><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>W</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>R</mi><mi>T</mi></msup></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mi>F</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><munder><mi>min</mi><mrow><msup><mi>Y</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>W</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow></munder><mo>⁡</mo><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>−</mo><mrow><msup><mi>L</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>Y</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>W</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>R</mi><mrow><mo rspace="0em">′</mo><mphantom></mphantom><mi>T</mi></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mi>F</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\max_{X}|\min_{Y,W}\|X-LYWR^{T}\|_{F}^{2}-\min_{Y^{\prime},W^{\prime}}\|X-L^{\prime}Y^{\prime}W^{\prime}R^{\prime T}\|_{F}^{2}|</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(24)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A1.p10.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.p10.2.1" class="ltx_text">Now, note that <math id="A1.p10.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\min_{Y,W}\|X-L^{\prime}Y^{\prime}W^{\prime}R^{\prime T}\|_{F}^{2}\leq\|X-L^{\prime}YWR^{\prime T}\|_{F}^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>min</mi><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>W</mi></mrow></msub><mo>⁡</mo><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>−</mo><mrow><msup><mi>L</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>Y</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>W</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>R</mi><mrow><mo rspace="0em">′</mo><mphantom></mphantom><mi>T</mi></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mi>F</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow><mo>≤</mo><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>−</mo><mrow><msup><mi>L</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>W</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>R</mi><mrow><mo rspace="0em">′</mo><mphantom></mphantom><mi>T</mi></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mi>F</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\min_{Y,W}\|X-L^{\prime}Y^{\prime}W^{\prime}R^{\prime T}\|_{F}^{2}\leq\|X-L^{\prime}YWR^{\prime T}\|_{F}^{2}</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="A1.p10.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(Y,W)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>W</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(Y,W)</annotation></semantics></math> minimizes the
<math id="A1.p10.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(L,R)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>L</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>R</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(L,R)</annotation></semantics></math> term in the above expression. Thus, if we can upper bound</span></p>
<table id="A1.EGx19" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="A1.E25"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A1.E25.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle|\|X-LYWR^{T}\|_{F}^{2}-\|X-L^{\prime}YWR^{\prime T}\|_{F}^{2}|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>W</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>R</mi><mi>T</mi></msup></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mi>F</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo>−</mo><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>−</mo><mrow><msup><mi>L</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>W</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>R</mi><mrow><mo rspace="0em">′</mo><mphantom></mphantom><mi>T</mi></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mi>F</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle|\|X-LYWR^{T}\|_{F}^{2}-\|X-L^{\prime}YWR^{\prime T}\|_{F}^{2}|</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(25)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A1.p10.3" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.p10.3.1" class="ltx_text">for arbitrary <math id="A1.p10.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y,W" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>W</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y,W</annotation></semantics></math>, this gives us an upper bound on (<a href="#A1.E24" title="In Proof. ‣ Appendix A Proof of Theorem ‣ Sparse Coding Representation of 2-way Data" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">24</span></a>). We next have</span></p>
<table id="A1.EGx20" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="A1.E26"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A1.E26.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle|\|X-LYWR^{T}\|_{F}^{2}-\|X-L^{\prime}YWR^{\prime T}\|_{F}^{2}|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>W</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>R</mi><mi>T</mi></msup></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mi>F</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo>−</mo><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>−</mo><mrow><msup><mi>L</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>W</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>R</mi><mrow><mo rspace="0em">′</mo><mphantom></mphantom><mi>T</mi></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mi>F</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle|\|X-LYWR^{T}\|_{F}^{2}-\|X-L^{\prime}YWR^{\prime T}\|_{F}^{2}|</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(26)</span></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="A1.E27"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A1.E27.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\leq 2|\|X-LYWR^{T}\|_{F}-\|X-L^{\prime}YWR^{\prime T}\|_{F}|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>W</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>R</mi><mi>T</mi></msup></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mi>F</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>−</mo><mrow><msup><mi>L</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>W</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>R</mi><mrow><mo rspace="0em">′</mo><mphantom></mphantom><mi>T</mi></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mi>F</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\leq 2|\|X-LYWR^{T}\|_{F}-\|X-L^{\prime}YWR^{\prime T}\|_{F}|</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(27)</span></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="A1.E28"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A1.E28.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\leq 2\|LYWR^{T}-L^{\prime}YWR^{\prime T}\|_{F}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>W</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>R</mi><mi>T</mi></msup></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msup><mi>L</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>W</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>R</mi><mrow><mo rspace="0em">′</mo><mphantom></mphantom><mi>T</mi></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mi>F</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\leq 2\|LYWR^{T}-L^{\prime}YWR^{\prime T}\|_{F}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(28)</span></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="A1.E29"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A1.E29.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\leq 2\|(L,R)-(L^{\prime},R^{\prime})\|_{op,1,2}\cdot\|YW\|_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>L</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>R</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>L</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>R</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>W</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\leq 2\|(L,R)-(L^{\prime},R^{\prime})\|_{op,1,2}\cdot\|YW\|_{1}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(29)</span></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="A1.E30"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A1.E30.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\leq 2\cdot\|(L,R)-(L^{\prime},R^{\prime})\|_{op,1,2}\cdot\|Y\|_{1}\|W\|_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>L</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>R</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>L</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>R</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msub><mo rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mrow><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mi>W</mi><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\leq 2\cdot\|(L,R)-(L^{\prime},R^{\prime})\|_{op,1,2}\cdot\|Y\|_{1}\|W\|_{1}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(30)</span></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="A1.E31"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A1.E31.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\leq 2\cdot\kappa^{2}\|(L,R)-(L^{\prime},R^{\prime})\|_{op,1,2}." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><msup><mi>κ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>L</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>R</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>L</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>R</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\leq 2\cdot\kappa^{2}\|(L,R)-(L^{\prime},R^{\prime})\|_{op,1,2}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(31)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A1.p10.4" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.p10.4.1" class="ltx_text">This implies the claimed Lipschitz bound.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="A1.p11" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A1.p11.1" class="ltx_p">Applying Lemma <a href="#Thmlemma2" title="Lemma 2 (Covering numbers of Banach space balls (Proposition 5 of [])). ‣ Appendix A Proof of Theorem ‣ Sparse Coding Representation of 2-way Data" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>, Lemma <a href="#Thmlemma3" title="Lemma 3 (Lipschitz bound for covering numbers). ‣ Appendix A Proof of Theorem ‣ Sparse Coding Representation of 2-way Data" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>, and Lemma <a href="#Thmlemma4" title="Lemma 4 (Lipschitz constant bound for the hypothesis to loss function mapping). ‣ Appendix A Proof of Theorem ‣ Sparse Coding Representation of 2-way Data" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a> we get the following bound on the covering numbers of <math id="A1.p11.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{F}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{F}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmproposition1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_proposition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmproposition1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Proposition 1</span></span><span id="Thmproposition1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Upper bound on the covering numbers of <math id="Thmproposition1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{F}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{F}</annotation></semantics></math>)<span id="Thmproposition1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmproposition1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmproposition1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmproposition1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">We have the following bound on the covering numbers of <math id="Thmproposition1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{F}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{F}</annotation></semantics></math>:</span></p>
<table id="A1.EGx21" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="A1.E32"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A1.E32.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\log\mathcal{N}(\mathcal{F},\gamma)\leq\log\mathcal{N}(\mathcal{H},\gamma/(2\kappa^{2}))=(NP+MQ)\log\left(\frac{8\kappa^{2}}{\gamma}\right)." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>γ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℋ</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>γ</mi><mo>/</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>κ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>P</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>Q</mi></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><mn>8</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>κ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mi>γ</mi></mfrac></mstyle><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\log\mathcal{N}(\mathcal{F},\gamma)\leq\log\mathcal{N}(\mathcal{H},\gamma/(2\kappa^{2}))=(NP+MQ)\log\left(\frac{8\kappa^{2}}{\gamma}\right).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(32)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
<div id="A1.p12" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A1.p12.1" class="ltx_p">Applying the bound in Proposition <a href="#Thmproposition1" title="Proposition 1 (Upper bound on the covering numbers of ℱ). ‣ Appendix A Proof of Theorem ‣ Sparse Coding Representation of 2-way Data" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> to Lemma <a href="#Thmlemma1" title="Lemma 1 (Generalization bound based on covering numbers of the loss class []). ‣ Appendix A Proof of Theorem ‣ Sparse Coding Representation of 2-way Data" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>,
we finally get the generalization bound given in Theorem <a href="#Thmtheorem1" title="Theorem 1 (Generalization bound for two-way dictionary learning). ‣ 4 Problem formulation and sample complexity ‣ Sparse Coding Representation of 2-way Data" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<div id="A1.p13" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A1.p13.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.p13.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Future theoretical directions inspired by the empirical analysis of the sample complexity bound.</span> Our experimental analysis of the bound (Fig. <a href="#S6.F3.sf1" title="In Figure 3 ‣ 6 Experimental evaluation ‣ Sparse Coding Representation of 2-way Data" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3(a)</span></a>) supports the main Theorem 2, which generalizes similar results for single-dictionary learning. However, the gap between the test error achieved by our algorithm and the bound motivates further theoretical investigation. This gap may be due to: (i) worst-case theoretical bound that holds for all data-generating distributions including the chosen distribution which may be particularly easy; (ii) the bound may be loose as a result of the fact that we do not exploit the low-rank constraint in the theoretical analysis. Since the bound is derived using known techniques and is of a similar form to the bound in the single-dictionary case, we regard our empirical analysis as providing motivation for derivation of data distribution-dependent sample complexity bounds for both the single- and multi-dictionary scenarios. Such bounds may be tighter for specific, natural distributions that arise in practice.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A2" class="ltx_appendix">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_appendix">Appendix B </span>Proof of Theorem <a href="#Thmtheorem2" title="Theorem 2 (Constrained optimization via regularization). ‣ 5 AODL: Dictionary learning for low-rank sparse coding ‣ Sparse Coding Representation of 2-way Data" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a></h2>

<div id="A2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Here we describe in detail the relationship between the reduction of the constrained optimization problem for sparse coding to the regularized one.</p>
</div>
<div id="A2.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">We start with a lemma.</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmlemma5" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_lemma">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmlemma5.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Lemma 5</span></span><span id="Thmlemma5.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(A solution to an <math id="Thmlemma5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> regularized problem is a solution for the constrained one)<span id="Thmlemma5.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmlemma5.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="Thmlemma5.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmlemma5.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Suppose that <math id="Thmlemma5.p1.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="f:\mathbb{R}^{d_{1}}\times\mathbb{R}^{d_{2}}\times\cdots\times\mathbb{R}^{d_{k}}\to\mathbb{R}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi>ℝ</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></msup><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>ℝ</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></msup><mo lspace="0.222em">×</mo><mo lspace="0.055em" rspace="0.055em">⋯</mo><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>ℝ</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></msup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>ℝ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f:\mathbb{R}^{d_{1}}\times\mathbb{R}^{d_{2}}\times\cdots\times\mathbb{R}^{d_{k}}\to\mathbb{R}</annotation></semantics></math>.
For <math id="Thmlemma5.p1.m2" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\gamma\in[0,\infty)^{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>γ</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>k</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma\in[0,\infty)^{k}</annotation></semantics></math>, define</span></p>
<table id="A2.EGx22" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="A2.E33"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A2.E33.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\displaystyle F(\gamma):=\argmin_{(x_{1},...,x_{k})\in\mathbb{R}^{d_{1}}\times\cdots\times\mathbb{R}^{d_{k}}}f(x)+\sum_{j=1}^{k}\gamma_{j}\|x_{j}\|_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>F</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>γ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>:=</mo><msub><mrow><mi>arg</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>min</mi></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>x</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo>∈</mo><mi>ℝ</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></msup><mo lspace="0.222em">×</mo><mo lspace="0.055em" rspace="0.055em">⋯</mo><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>ℝ</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></msup></mrow></msub><mi>f</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>+</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>k</mi></munderover></mstyle><msub><mi>γ</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∥</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><msub><mo lspace="0em">∥</mo><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle F(\gamma):=\argmin_{(x_{1},...,x_{k})\in\mathbb{R}^{d_{1}}\times\cdots\times\mathbb{R}^{d_{k}}}f(x)+\sum_{j=1}^{k}\gamma_{j}\|x_{j}\|_{1}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(33)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="Thmlemma5.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmlemma5.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">and</span></p>
<table id="A2.EGx23" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="A2.E34"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A2.E34.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\displaystyle G(\kappa):=\argmin_{x_{1}^{k}\in\mathbb{R}^{d_{1}}\times\cdots\times\mathbb{R}^{d_{k}}~|~\forall j\|x_{j}\|_{1}\leq\kappa}f(x_{1}^{k})." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>G</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>κ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>:=</mo><msub><mrow><mi>arg</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>min</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>x</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mn>1</mn></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><mi>k</mi></msup><mo>∈</mo><mi>ℝ</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></msup><mo lspace="0.222em">×</mo><mo lspace="0.055em" rspace="0.055em">⋯</mo><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>ℝ</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></msup><mo fence="false" rspace="0.437em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mo rspace="0.167em">∀</mo><mi>j</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∥</mo><mi>x</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>j</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.0835em">∥</mo><msub><mrow></mrow><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0.0835em">≤</mo><mi>κ</mi></mrow></msub><mi>f</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn><mi>k</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle G(\kappa):=\argmin_{x_{1}^{k}\in\mathbb{R}^{d_{1}}\times\cdots\times\mathbb{R}^{d_{k}}~|~\forall j\|x_{j}\|_{1}\leq\kappa}f(x_{1}^{k}).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(34)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="Thmlemma5.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="Thmlemma5.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmlemma5.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Then for every <math id="Thmlemma5.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma\geq\bf{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>γ</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>𝟎</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma\geq\bf{0}</annotation></semantics></math>, there exists a <math id="Thmlemma5.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\kappa" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>κ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\kappa</annotation></semantics></math> such that
<math id="Thmlemma5.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F(\gamma)\subseteq G(\kappa)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>γ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>⊆</mo><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>κ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F(\gamma)\subseteq G(\kappa)</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="A2.2" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="A2.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A2.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A2.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text">Let <math id="A2.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x^{(0)}\in F(\gamma)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>x</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>γ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x^{(0)}\in F(\gamma)</annotation></semantics></math> for some <math id="A2.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>γ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma</annotation></semantics></math>. Set <math id="A2.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\kappa_{j}:=\|x_{j}^{(0)}\|_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>κ</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>:=</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><msubsup><mi>x</mi><mi>j</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\kappa_{j}:=\|x_{j}^{(0)}\|_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> for all <math id="A2.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>j</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j</annotation></semantics></math>. Let <math id="A2.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x^{(1)}\in G(\kappa)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>x</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>κ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x^{(1)}\in G(\kappa)</annotation></semantics></math>.
By optimality of <math id="A2.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x^{(0)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>x</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x^{(0)}</annotation></semantics></math> for the regularized problem, we have</span></p>
<table id="A2.EGx24" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="A2.E35"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A2.E35.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle f(x^{(0)})+\sum_{j=1}^{k}\gamma_{j}\|x_{j}^{(0)}\|_{1}\leq f(x^{(1)})+\sum_{j=1}^{k}\gamma_{j}\|x_{j}^{(1)}\|_{1}." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>k</mi></munderover></mstyle><mrow><msub><mi>γ</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><msubsup><mi>x</mi><mi>j</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>k</mi></munderover></mstyle><mrow><msub><mi>γ</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><msubsup><mi>x</mi><mi>j</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle f(x^{(0)})+\sum_{j=1}^{k}\gamma_{j}\|x_{j}^{(0)}\|_{1}\leq f(x^{(1)})+\sum_{j=1}^{k}\gamma_{j}\|x_{j}^{(1)}\|_{1}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(35)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A2.p3.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="A2.p3.2.1" class="ltx_text">By optimality of <math id="A2.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x^{(1)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>x</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x^{(1)}</annotation></semantics></math> for the constrained problem, we have <math id="A2.p3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\|x_{j}^{(1)}\|_{1}\leq\kappa_{j}=\|x_{j}^{(0)}\|_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><msubsup><mi>x</mi><mi>j</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>≤</mo><msub><mi>κ</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><msubsup><mi>x</mi><mi>j</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\|x_{j}^{(1)}\|_{1}\leq\kappa_{j}=\|x_{j}^{(0)}\|_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> for all <math id="A2.p3.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>j</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j</annotation></semantics></math>.
This implies by (<a href="#A2.E35" title="In Proof. ‣ Appendix B Proof of Theorem ‣ Sparse Coding Representation of 2-way Data" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">35</span></a>) that</span></p>
<table id="A2.EGx25" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="A2.E36"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A2.E36.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle f(x^{(0)})+\sum_{j=1}^{k}\gamma_{j}\|x^{(0)}_{j}\|_{1}\leq f(x^{(1)})+\sum_{j=1}^{k}\gamma_{j}\|x^{(0)}_{j}\|_{1}," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>k</mi></munderover></mstyle><mrow><msub><mi>γ</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><msubsup><mi>x</mi><mi>j</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>k</mi></munderover></mstyle><mrow><msub><mi>γ</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><msubsup><mi>x</mi><mi>j</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle f(x^{(0)})+\sum_{j=1}^{k}\gamma_{j}\|x^{(0)}_{j}\|_{1}\leq f(x^{(1)})+\sum_{j=1}^{k}\gamma_{j}\|x^{(0)}_{j}\|_{1},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(36)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A2.p3.3" class="ltx_p"><span id="A2.p3.3.1" class="ltx_text">which implies</span></p>
<table id="A2.EGx26" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="A2.E37"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A2.E37.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle f(x^{(0)})\leq f(x^{(1)})." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle f(x^{(0)})\leq f(x^{(1)}).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(37)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A2.p3.4" class="ltx_p"><span id="A2.p3.4.1" class="ltx_text">Since <math id="A2.p3.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x^{(0)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>x</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x^{(0)}</annotation></semantics></math> is feasible for the constrained problem, this implies that <math id="A2.p3.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x^{(0)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>x</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x^{(0)}</annotation></semantics></math> is a solution for it,
meaning that <math id="A2.p3.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x^{(0)}\in G(\kappa)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>x</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>κ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x^{(0)}\in G(\kappa)</annotation></semantics></math>, as desired.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="A2.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A2.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text">We have thus shown that for each <math id="A2.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>γ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma</annotation></semantics></math>, there exists <math id="A2.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\kappa" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>κ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\kappa</annotation></semantics></math> such that
<math id="A2.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F(\gamma)\subseteq G(\kappa)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>γ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>⊆</mo><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>κ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F(\gamma)\subseteq G(\kappa)</annotation></semantics></math>. Note that we did not use convexity of <math id="A2.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>f</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f</annotation></semantics></math> anywhere.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="A2.p5" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A2.p5.1" class="ltx_p">Now, it is easy to check the following monotonicity property of <math id="A2.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F(\gamma)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>γ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F(\gamma)</annotation></semantics></math>:
if <math id="A2.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma_{j}\leq\hat{\gamma}_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>γ</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>≤</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>γ</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma_{j}\leq\hat{\gamma}_{j}</annotation></semantics></math> for each <math id="A2.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>j</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j</annotation></semantics></math>, and if we define
<math id="A2.p5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\kappa:=\min\{k~|~F(\gamma)\subseteq G(k)\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>κ</mi><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mi>min</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mi>k</mi><mo lspace="0.330em" rspace="0.330em">|</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>γ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>⊆</mo><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\kappa:=\min\{k~|~F(\gamma)\subseteq G(k)\}</annotation></semantics></math>
and <math id="A2.p5.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{\kappa}:=\min\{k~|~F(\hat{\gamma})\subseteq G(k)\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>κ</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mi>min</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mi>k</mi><mo lspace="0.330em" rspace="0.330em">|</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>γ</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>⊆</mo><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{\kappa}:=\min\{k~|~F(\hat{\gamma})\subseteq G(k)\}</annotation></semantics></math>, then</p>
<table id="A2.EGx27" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="A2.E38"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A2.E38.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\kappa\geq\hat{\kappa}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>κ</mi><mo>≥</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>κ</mi><mo>^</mo></mover></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\kappa\geq\hat{\kappa}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(38)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A2.p5.2" class="ltx_p">and</p>
<table id="A2.EGx28" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="A2.E39"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A2.E39.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle F(\hat{\gamma})\subseteq F(\gamma)." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>γ</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>⊆</mo><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>γ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle F(\hat{\gamma})\subseteq F(\gamma).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(39)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A2.p5.3" class="ltx_p">Moreover, setting <math id="A2.p5.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma=\bf{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>γ</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>𝟎</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma=\bf{0}</annotation></semantics></math> eliminates the constraint, meaning that
<math id="A2.p5.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F(\bf{0})\subseteq G(\infty)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>𝟎</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>⊆</mo><mrow><mi>𝐆</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F(\bf{0})\subseteq G(\infty)</annotation></semantics></math>.
This allows for a bisection search to select each regularization parameter to enforce desired
constraints. It also has the following consequence: for every <math id="A2.p5.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\kappa&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>κ</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\kappa&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math>, there exists
a <math id="A2.p5.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>γ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma</annotation></semantics></math> for which <math id="A2.p5.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F(\gamma)\subseteq G(\kappa)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>γ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>⊆</mo><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>κ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F(\gamma)\subseteq G(\kappa)</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="A2.p6" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A2.p6.1" class="ltx_p">We can apply Lemma <a href="#Thmlemma5" title="Lemma 5 (A solution to an 𝐿_1 regularized problem is a solution for the constrained one). ‣ Appendix B Proof of Theorem ‣ Sparse Coding Representation of 2-way Data" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a> to our problem formulation as follows to show that it can be solved via the regularization approach.
The constrained problem takes the form</p>
<table id="A2.EGx29" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="A2.E40"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A2.E40.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\displaystyle\argmin_{L,R}\sum_{j=1}^{S}\ell((L,R),X_{j})=\argmin_{L,R}\sum_{j=1}^{S}\min_{Y_{j},W_{j}~:~\|Y_{j}\|_{1},\|W_{j}\|_{1}\leq\kappa}\|X_{j}-LY_{j}W_{j}R^{T}\|_{F}^{2}." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mrow><mi>arg</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>min</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>R</mi></mrow></msub><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>S</mi></munderover></mstyle><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>L</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>R</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><msub><mrow><mi>arg</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>min</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>R</mi></mrow></msub><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>S</mi></munderover></mstyle><munder><mi>min</mi><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>W</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.548em">:</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><msub><mi>W</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>≤</mo><mi>κ</mi></mrow></mrow></munder><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∥</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>LY</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><msub><mi>W</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><msup><mi>R</mi><mi>T</mi></msup><msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.0835em">∥</mo><mi>F</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo lspace="0.0835em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\argmin_{L,R}\sum_{j=1}^{S}\ell((L,R),X_{j})=\argmin_{L,R}\sum_{j=1}^{S}\min_{Y_{j},W_{j}~:~\|Y_{j}\|_{1},\|W_{j}\|_{1}\leq\kappa}\|X_{j}-LY_{j}W_{j}R^{T}\|_{F}^{2}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(40)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A2.p6.2" class="ltx_p">Using Lemma <a href="#Thmlemma5" title="Lemma 5 (A solution to an 𝐿_1 regularized problem is a solution for the constrained one). ‣ Appendix B Proof of Theorem ‣ Sparse Coding Representation of 2-way Data" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a>, we can solve the inner minimization by solving the regularized version: defining</p>
<table id="A2.EGx30" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="A2.E41"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A2.E41.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle Y_{*,j},W_{*,j}:=\argmin_{Y_{j},W_{j}}\|X_{j}-LYWR^{T}\|_{F}^{2}+\lambda_{1}\|Y_{j}\|_{1}+\lambda_{2}\|W_{j}\|_{1}," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>Y</mi><mrow><mo rspace="0em">∗</mo><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>W</mi><mrow><mo rspace="0em">∗</mo><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mrow><mi>arg</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>min</mi></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>W</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow></msub><mo>⁡</mo><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><msup><mi>LYWR</mi><mi>T</mi></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mi>F</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>λ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>λ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><msub><mi>W</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle Y_{*,j},W_{*,j}:=\argmin_{Y_{j},W_{j}}\|X_{j}-LYWR^{T}\|_{F}^{2}+\lambda_{1}\|Y_{j}\|_{1}+\lambda_{2}\|W_{j}\|_{1},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(41)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A2.p6.3" class="ltx_p">for appropriately chosen <math id="A2.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda_{1}(L,R,X_{j})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>λ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>L</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>R</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda_{1}(L,R,X_{j})</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A2.p6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda_{2}(L,R,X_{j})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>λ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>L</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>R</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda_{2}(L,R,X_{j})</annotation></semantics></math>,
we get that</p>
<table id="A2.EGx31" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="A2.E42"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A2.E42.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\displaystyle(\ref{expr:constrained})=\argmin_{L,R}\sum_{j=1}^{S}\|X_{j}-LY_{*,j}W_{*,j}R^{T}\|_{F}^{2}." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic"><a href="#A2.E40" title="In Appendix B Proof of Theorem ‣ Sparse Coding Representation of 2-way Data" class="ltx_ref ltx_font_italic"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">40</span></a></mtext><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><msub><mrow><mi>arg</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>min</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>R</mi></mrow></msub><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>S</mi></munderover></mstyle><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∥</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>LY</mi><mrow><mo rspace="0em">∗</mo><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><msub><mi>W</mi><mrow><mo rspace="0em">∗</mo><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><msup><mi>R</mi><mi>T</mi></msup><msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.0835em">∥</mo><mi>F</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo lspace="0.0835em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle(\ref{expr:constrained})=\argmin_{L,R}\sum_{j=1}^{S}\|X_{j}-LY_{*,j}W_{*,j}R^{T}\|_{F}^{2}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(42)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="A2.p7" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A2.p7.1" class="ltx_p">This shows that solving the sparse coding problem via the regularized objective function provides an exact solution to the original problem, which completes the proof.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A3" class="ltx_appendix">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_appendix">Appendix C </span>Proof of Theorem <a href="#Thmtheorem3" title="Theorem 3 (Convergence of AODL). ‣ 5 AODL: Dictionary learning for low-rank sparse coding ‣ Sparse Coding Representation of 2-way Data" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a></h2>

<div id="A3.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Here we prove that the sequence of objective function values in our alternating minimization scheme converges. This follows from the fact that any monotone decreasing sequence of real numbers that is bounded below converges to its infimum. To apply this to our case, we simply establish that the sequence of iterates of the dictionaries <math id="A3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(L^{(k)},R^{(k)})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>L</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>R</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(L^{(k)},R^{(k)})</annotation></semantics></math> and the sparse coding matrices <math id="A3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(Y^{(k)},W^{(k)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>Y</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>W</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup></mrow><mo fence="true" lspace="0em">CLOSE</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(Y^{(k)},W^{(k)}</annotation></semantics></math> yields a monotone decreasing sequence of objective function values. The objective function is bounded below by <math id="A3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0</annotation></semantics></math>, and so this establishes convergence.</p>
</div>
<div id="A3.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">For convenience, we introduce notation for the objective function:
we denote the objective of the inner minimization by <math id="A3.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="J_{coding,s}(X,L,R,Y,W)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>J</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>L</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>R</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>W</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">J_{coding,s}(X,L,R,Y,W)</annotation></semantics></math>, and then the entire objective function becomes</p>
<table id="A3.EGx32" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="A3.E43"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A3.E43.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle J(X,L,R,Y,W):=\sum_{s=1}^{S}J_{coding,s}(X,L,R,Y,W)." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>J</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>L</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>R</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>W</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>S</mi></munderover></mstyle><mrow><msub><mi>J</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>L</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>R</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>W</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle J(X,L,R,Y,W):=\sum_{s=1}^{S}J_{coding,s}(X,L,R,Y,W).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(43)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A3.p2.2" class="ltx_p">For fixed <math id="A3.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L,R,X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>R</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>X</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L,R,X</annotation></semantics></math>, replacing <math id="A3.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y_{s},W_{s}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>W</mi><mi>s</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y_{s},W_{s}</annotation></semantics></math> by <math id="A3.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\argmin_{Y_{s},W_{s}}J_{coding,s}(X,L,R,Y,W)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mrow><mi>arg</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>min</mi></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>W</mi><mi>s</mi></msub></mrow></msub><mo>⁡</mo><msub><mi>J</mi><mrow><mi>coding</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>L</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>R</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>W</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\argmin_{Y_{s},W_{s}}J_{coding,s}(X,L,R,Y,W)</annotation></semantics></math> decreases the value of the objective, and the same holds when we replace <math id="A3.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L,R" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>R</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L,R</annotation></semantics></math> by
<math id="A3.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\argmin_{L,R}J(X,L,R,Y,W)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mrow><mi>arg</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>min</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>R</mi></mrow></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mi>J</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>L</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>R</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>W</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\argmin_{L,R}J(X,L,R,Y,W)</annotation></semantics></math>.
Thus, the value of the objective function is non-increasing in each iteration of the algorithm. This implies convergence of the sequence of objective function values, which completes the proof.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A4" class="ltx_appendix">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_appendix">Appendix D </span><span id="A4.2" class="ltx_text ltx_markedasmath">AODL</span> algorithm and derivation details</h2>

<div id="A4.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A4.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Initialization of <math id="A4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>L</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>R</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R</annotation></semantics></math>.</span>
We experiment with two different approaches to initialize the dictionaries in Step 3 of Alg. <a href="#alg1" title="Algorithm 1 ‣ 5 AODL: Dictionary learning for low-rank sparse coding ‣ Sparse Coding Representation of 2-way Data" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>: normally random and on tensor decomposition. In the former approach we sample each element interdependently from a normal distribution <math id="A4.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{N}(0,1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{N}(0,1)</annotation></semantics></math>, and then normalize atoms to unit length. In the latter, we stack all samples in a 3-way tensor and employ Tucker decomposition, which decomposes a tensor into a product of 3 factor matrices and a core tensor with user-defined inner dimensions. We use the mode-1 factor with selected second dimension <math id="A4.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>P</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P</annotation></semantics></math> to initialize <math id="A4.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>L</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L</annotation></semantics></math>, and mode-2 factor with selected second dimension <math id="A4.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q</annotation></semantics></math> to initialize <math id="A4.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>R</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R</annotation></semantics></math>. Note that the Tucker initialization of the dictionaries works only when we are aiming to learn a complete or under-complete dictionaries since Tucker requires <math id="A4.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P\leq N,Q\leq M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>≤</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>Q</mi><mo>≤</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P\leq N,Q\leq M</annotation></semantics></math>.
We employed random initialization for all experiments (and all baselines that learn dictionaries) with synthetic data; and tensor decomposition initialization for all real-world experiments (and all baselines that learn dictionaries). These decisions were based on empirically faster convergence (fewer iterations) for all competing methods with the corresponding initialization schemes.</p>
</div>
<div id="A4.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A4.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A4.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Stage I: Sparse coding.</span> Given fixed dictionaries, we estimate the sparse coding coefficients one sample at a time. Since for any <math id="A4.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{s}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{s}</annotation></semantics></math>, the problem is jointly convex, we can employ the Alternating Direction Method of Multipliers (ADMM) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">7</a>]</cite> to solve for <math id="A4.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y_{s},W_{s}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>W</mi><mi>s</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y_{s},W_{s}</annotation></semantics></math> similar to <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib27" title="" class="ltx_ref">27</a>]</cite>. We first introduce intermediate variables <math id="A4.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U_{s}=Y_{s},V_{s}=W_{s}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>U</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>s</mi></msub></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>W</mi><mi>s</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U_{s}=Y_{s},V_{s}=W_{s}</annotation></semantics></math>, and rewrite the objective for sample <math id="A4.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>s</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s</annotation></semantics></math> as:</p>
</div>
<div id="A4.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<table id="A4.E44" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_table">
<tbody>
<tr id="A4.E44X" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A4.E44X.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\underset{Y_{s},W_{s},U_{s},V_{s}}{\mathrm{argmin}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><munder accentunder="true"><mi>argmin</mi><mrow><msub><mi mathsize="0.700em">Y</mi><mi mathsize="0.710em">s</mi></msub><mo mathsize="0.700em">,</mo><msub><mi mathsize="0.700em">W</mi><mi mathsize="0.710em">s</mi></msub><mo mathsize="0.700em">,</mo><msub><mi mathsize="0.700em">U</mi><mi mathsize="0.710em">s</mi></msub><mo mathsize="0.700em">,</mo><msub><mi mathsize="0.700em">V</mi><mi mathsize="0.710em">s</mi></msub></mrow></munder><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\underset{Y_{s},W_{s},U_{s},V_{s}}{\mathrm{argmin}}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A4.E44X.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle||X_{s}-LY_{s}W_{s}R^{T}||_{F}^{2}+\lambda_{1}||U_{s}||_{1}+\lambda_{2}||V_{s}||_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>W</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>R</mi><mi>T</mi></msup></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mi>F</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>λ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><msub><mi>U</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>λ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><mo fence="false">|</mo><msub><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle||X_{s}-LY_{s}W_{s}R^{T}||_{F}^{2}+\lambda_{1}||U_{s}||_{1}+\lambda_{2}||V_{s}||_{1}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="2" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equationgroup ltx_align_right">(44)</span></td></tr>
<tr id="A4.E44Xa" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A4.E44Xa.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle s.t." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">.</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle s.t.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A4.E44Xa.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle Y_{s}=U_{s},W_{s}=V_{s}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>U</mi><mi>s</mi></msub></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>W</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>s</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle Y_{s}=U_{s},W_{s}=V_{s}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="A4.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A4.p4.1" class="ltx_p">We form the corresponding Lagrangian function:</p>
</div>
<div id="A4.p5" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<table id="A4.E45" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_table">
<tbody>
<tr id="A4.E45X" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A4.E45X.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\mathcal{L}(Y_{s},W_{s},U_{s},V_{s})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>W</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>U</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mathcal{L}(Y_{s},W_{s},U_{s},V_{s})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="2" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equationgroup ltx_align_right">(45)</span></td></tr>
<tr id="A4.E45Xa" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A4.E45Xa.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>=</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A4.E45Xa.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle||X_{s}-LY_{s}W_{s}R^{T}||_{F}^{2}+\lambda_{1}||U_{s}||_{1}+\lambda_{2}||V_{s}||_{1}+\frac{\rho_{1}}{2}||U_{s}-Y_{s}+\frac{\Gamma_{1}}{\rho_{1}}||_{F}^{2}+\frac{\rho_{2}}{2}||V_{s}-W_{s}+\frac{\Gamma_{2}}{\rho_{2}}||_{F}^{2}." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><msubsup><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>W</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>R</mi><mi>T</mi></msup></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mi>F</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo>+</mo><msubsup><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>λ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><msub><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi>U</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>λ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><msub><mi>ρ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mstyle></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>U</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>s</mi></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><msub><mi>ρ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mfrac></mstyle></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mi>F</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo>+</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><msub><mi>ρ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mstyle></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>W</mi><mi>s</mi></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><msub><mi>ρ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mfrac></mstyle></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mi>F</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle||X_{s}-LY_{s}W_{s}R^{T}||_{F}^{2}+\lambda_{1}||U_{s}||_{1}+\lambda_{2}||V_{s}||_{1}+\frac{\rho_{1}}{2}||U_{s}-Y_{s}+\frac{\Gamma_{1}}{\rho_{1}}||_{F}^{2}+\frac{\rho_{2}}{2}||V_{s}-W_{s}+\frac{\Gamma_{2}}{\rho_{2}}||_{F}^{2}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="A4.p6" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A4.p6.2" class="ltx_p">We alternate between direct updates of <math id="A4.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y_{s},W_{s},U_{s}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>W</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>U</mi><mi>s</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y_{s},W_{s},U_{s}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A4.p6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V_{s}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V_{s}</annotation></semantics></math> obtained by setting gradients w.r.t. each variable to zero. To update <math id="A4.p6.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y_{s}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y_{s}</annotation></semantics></math>, we have the following optimization problem:</p>
<table id="A4.E46" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_table">
<tbody>
<tr id="A4.E46X" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A4.E46X.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\underset{Ys}{\mathrm{argmin}}||X_{s}-LY_{s}W_{s}R^{T}||_{F}^{2}+\frac{\rho_{1}}{2}||U_{s}-Y_{s}+\frac{\Gamma_{1}}{\rho_{1}}||_{F}^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><munder accentunder="true"><mi>argmin</mi><mrow><mi mathsize="0.700em">Y</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathsize="0.700em">s</mi></mrow></munder><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>W</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>R</mi><mi>T</mi></msup></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mi>F</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><msub><mi>ρ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>U</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>s</mi></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><msub><mi>ρ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mfrac></mstyle></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mi>F</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\underset{Ys}{\mathrm{argmin}}||X_{s}-LY_{s}W_{s}R^{T}||_{F}^{2}+\frac{\rho_{1}}{2}||U_{s}-Y_{s}+\frac{\Gamma_{1}}{\rho_{1}}||_{F}^{2}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equationgroup ltx_align_right">(46)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="A4.p7" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A4.p7.2" class="ltx_p">Setting the gradient with respect to <math id="A4.p7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y_{s}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y_{s}</annotation></semantics></math> to zero, we obtain:</p>
<table id="A4.E47" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_table">
<tbody>
<tr id="A4.E47X" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A4.E47X.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle 2L^{T}LY_{s}BB^{T}+\rho_{1}Y_{s}=2L^{T}X_{s}B^{T}+\rho_{1}U_{s}+\Gamma_{1}," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>L</mi><mi>T</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>L</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>B</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>B</mi><mi>T</mi></msup></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>ρ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>s</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>L</mi><mi>T</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>B</mi><mi>T</mi></msup></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>ρ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>U</mi><mi>s</mi></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle 2L^{T}LY_{s}BB^{T}+\rho_{1}Y_{s}=2L^{T}X_{s}B^{T}+\rho_{1}U_{s}+\Gamma_{1},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equationgroup ltx_align_right">(47)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A4.p7.3" class="ltx_p">where <math id="A4.p7.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B=W_{s}R^{T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>W</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>R</mi><mi>T</mi></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B=W_{s}R^{T}</annotation></semantics></math>.
To simplify we use the following eigendecompositions: <math id="A4.p7.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L^{T}L=Q_{1}\Lambda_{1}Q_{1}^{T},BB^{T}=Q_{2}\Lambda_{2}Q_{2}^{T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>L</mi><mi>T</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>L</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>Q</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>Q</mi><mn>1</mn><mi>T</mi></msubsup></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>B</mi><mi>T</mi></msup></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>Q</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>Q</mi><mn>2</mn><mi>T</mi></msubsup></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L^{T}L=Q_{1}\Lambda_{1}Q_{1}^{T},BB^{T}=Q_{2}\Lambda_{2}Q_{2}^{T}</annotation></semantics></math>, and set <math id="A4.p7.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Pi_{1}=2L^{T}X_{s}B^{T}+\rho_{1}U_{s}+\Gamma_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Π</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>L</mi><mi>T</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>B</mi><mi>T</mi></msup></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>ρ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>U</mi><mi>s</mi></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Pi_{1}=2L^{T}X_{s}B^{T}+\rho_{1}U_{s}+\Gamma_{1}</annotation></semantics></math>. We can then rewrite the above equation as:</p>
<table id="A4.E48" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_table">
<tbody>
<tr id="A4.E48X" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A4.E48X.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle 2L^{T}LY_{s}BB^{T}+\rho_{1}Y_{s}=" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>L</mi><mi>T</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>L</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>B</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>B</mi><mi>T</mi></msup></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>ρ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>s</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mphantom></mphantom></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle 2L^{T}LY_{s}BB^{T}+\rho_{1}Y_{s}=</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A4.E48X.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\Pi_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Π</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\Pi_{1}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="4" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equationgroup ltx_align_right">(48)</span></td></tr>
<tr id="A4.E48Xa" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A4.E48Xa.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle 2Q_{1}\Lambda_{1}Q_{1}^{T}Y_{s}Q_{2}\Lambda_{2}Q_{2}^{T}+\rho_{1}Y_{s}=" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>Q</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>Q</mi><mn>1</mn><mi>T</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>Q</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>Q</mi><mn>2</mn><mi>T</mi></msubsup></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>ρ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>s</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mphantom></mphantom></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle 2Q_{1}\Lambda_{1}Q_{1}^{T}Y_{s}Q_{2}\Lambda_{2}Q_{2}^{T}+\rho_{1}Y_{s}=</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A4.E48Xa.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\Pi_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Π</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\Pi_{1}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr>
<tr id="A4.E48Xb" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A4.E48Xb.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle 2Q_{1}^{T}Q_{1}\Lambda_{1}Q_{1}^{T}Y_{s}Q_{2}\Lambda_{2}Q_{2}^{T}Q_{2}+\rho_{1}Q_{1}^{T}Y_{s}Q_{2}=" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>Q</mi><mn>1</mn><mi>T</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>Q</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>Q</mi><mn>1</mn><mi>T</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>Q</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>Q</mi><mn>2</mn><mi>T</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>Q</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>ρ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>Q</mi><mn>1</mn><mi>T</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>Q</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mphantom></mphantom></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle 2Q_{1}^{T}Q_{1}\Lambda_{1}Q_{1}^{T}Y_{s}Q_{2}\Lambda_{2}Q_{2}^{T}Q_{2}+\rho_{1}Q_{1}^{T}Y_{s}Q_{2}=</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A4.E48Xb.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle Q_{1}^{T}\Pi_{1}Q_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>Q</mi><mn>1</mn><mi>T</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Π</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>Q</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle Q_{1}^{T}\Pi_{1}Q_{2}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr>
<tr id="A4.E48Xc" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A4.E48Xc.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle 2\Lambda_{1}Q_{1}^{T}Y_{s}Q_{2}\Lambda_{2}+\rho_{1}Q_{1}^{T}Y_{s}Q_{2}=" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>Q</mi><mn>1</mn><mi>T</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>Q</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>ρ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>Q</mi><mn>1</mn><mi>T</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>Q</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mphantom></mphantom></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle 2\Lambda_{1}Q_{1}^{T}Y_{s}Q_{2}\Lambda_{2}+\rho_{1}Q_{1}^{T}Y_{s}Q_{2}=</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A4.E48Xc.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle Q_{1}^{T}\Pi_{1}Q_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>Q</mi><mn>1</mn><mi>T</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Π</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>Q</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle Q_{1}^{T}\Pi_{1}Q_{2}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A4.p7.4" class="ltx_p">Allowing <math id="A4.p7.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E_{1}=Q_{1}^{T}Y_{s}Q_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>E</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>Q</mi><mn>1</mn><mi>T</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>Q</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E_{1}=Q_{1}^{T}Y_{s}Q_{2}</annotation></semantics></math>, we obtain <math id="A4.p7.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2\Lambda_{1}E_{1}\Lambda_{2}+\rho_{1}E_{1}=Q_{1}^{T}\Pi_{1}Q_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>E</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>ρ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>E</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>Q</mi><mn>1</mn><mi>T</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Π</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>Q</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2\Lambda_{1}E_{1}\Lambda_{2}+\rho_{1}E_{1}=Q_{1}^{T}\Pi_{1}Q_{2}</annotation></semantics></math>, and an element-wise solution for <math id="A4.p7.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>E</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> as follows:</p>
<table id="A4.E49" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_table">
<tbody>
<tr id="A4.E49X" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A4.E49X.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle{[E_{1}]}_{i,j}=\frac{{[Q_{1}^{T}\Pi_{1}Q_{2}]}_{i,j}}{2{[\Lambda_{1}]}_{i,i}{[\Lambda_{2}]}_{j,j}+\rho_{1}}," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msub><mi>E</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>Q</mi><mn>1</mn><mi>T</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Π</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>Q</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>ρ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></mfrac></mstyle></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle{[E_{1}]}_{i,j}=\frac{{[Q_{1}^{T}\Pi_{1}Q_{2}]}_{i,j}}{2{[\Lambda_{1}]}_{i,i}{[\Lambda_{2}]}_{j,j}+\rho_{1}},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equationgroup ltx_align_right">(49)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A4.p7.5" class="ltx_p">and <math id="A4.p7.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y_{s}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y_{s}</annotation></semantics></math> can then be recovered as <math id="A4.p7.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y_{s}=Q_{1}E_{1}Q_{2}^{T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>Q</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>E</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>Q</mi><mn>2</mn><mi>T</mi></msubsup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y_{s}=Q_{1}E_{1}Q_{2}^{T}</annotation></semantics></math>. We follow a similar procedure to derive an analogous update for <math id="A4.p7.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W_{s}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>W</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W_{s}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="A4.p8" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A4.p8.1" class="ltx_p">The optimization sub-problems for <math id="A4.p8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U_{s}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>U</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U_{s}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A4.p8.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V_{s}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V_{s}</annotation></semantics></math> are:</p>
<table id="A4.E50" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_table">
<tbody>
<tr id="A4.E50X" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A4.E50X.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\underset{U_{s}}{\mathrm{argmin}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><munder accentunder="true"><mi>argmin</mi><msub><mi mathsize="0.700em">U</mi><mi mathsize="0.710em">s</mi></msub></munder><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\underset{U_{s}}{\mathrm{argmin}}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A4.E50X.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\lambda_{1}||U_{s}||_{1}+\frac{\rho_{1}}{2}||U_{s}-Y_{s}+\frac{\Gamma_{1}}{\rho_{1}}||_{F}^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>λ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><msub><mi>U</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><msub><mi>ρ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>U</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>s</mi></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><msub><mi>ρ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mfrac></mstyle></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mi>F</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\lambda_{1}||U_{s}||_{1}+\frac{\rho_{1}}{2}||U_{s}-Y_{s}+\frac{\Gamma_{1}}{\rho_{1}}||_{F}^{2}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="2" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equationgroup ltx_align_right">(50)</span></td></tr>
<tr id="A4.E50Xa" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A4.E50Xa.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\underset{V_{s}}{\mathrm{argmin}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><munder accentunder="true"><mi>argmin</mi><msub><mi mathsize="0.700em">V</mi><mi mathsize="0.710em">s</mi></msub></munder><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\underset{V_{s}}{\mathrm{argmin}}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A4.E50Xa.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\lambda_{2}||V_{s}||_{1}+\frac{\rho_{2}}{2}||V_{s}-W_{s}+\frac{\Gamma_{2}}{\rho_{2}}||_{F}^{2}," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>λ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><msub><mi>ρ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>W</mi><mi>s</mi></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><msub><mi>ρ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mfrac></mstyle></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mi>F</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\lambda_{2}||V_{s}||_{1}+\frac{\rho_{2}}{2}||V_{s}-W_{s}+\frac{\Gamma_{2}}{\rho_{2}}||_{F}^{2},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A4.p8.2" class="ltx_p">with existing closed-form solutions due to <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib23" title="" class="ltx_ref">23</a>]</cite>:</p>
<table id="A4.E51" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_table">
<tbody>
<tr id="A4.E51X" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A4.E51X.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle{[U_{s}]}_{i,j}=" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msub><mi>U</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mphantom></mphantom></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle{[U_{s}]}_{i,j}=</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A4.E51X.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\text{sign}({[H_{1}]}_{i,j})\times\max{(|{[H_{1}]}_{i,j}|-\frac{\lambda_{1}}{\rho_{1}},0)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mtext>sign</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mrow><mi>max</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>−</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><msub><mi>λ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><msub><mi>ρ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mfrac></mstyle></mrow><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\text{sign}({[H_{1}]}_{i,j})\times\max{(|{[H_{1}]}_{i,j}|-\frac{\lambda_{1}}{\rho_{1}},0)}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="2" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equationgroup ltx_align_right">(51)</span></td></tr>
<tr id="A4.E51Xa" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A4.E51Xa.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle{[V_{s}]}_{i,j}=" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mphantom></mphantom></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle{[V_{s}]}_{i,j}=</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A4.E51Xa.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\text{sign}({[H_{2}]}_{i,j})\times\max{(|{[H_{2}]}_{i,j}|-\frac{\lambda_{2}}{\rho_{2}},0)}," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>sign</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mrow><mi>max</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>−</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><msub><mi>λ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><msub><mi>ρ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mfrac></mstyle></mrow><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\text{sign}({[H_{2}]}_{i,j})\times\max{(|{[H_{2}]}_{i,j}|-\frac{\lambda_{2}}{\rho_{2}},0)},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A4.p8.3" class="ltx_p">where <math id="A4.p8.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H_{1}=Y_{s}-\frac{\Gamma_{1}}{\rho_{1}},H_{2}=W_{s}-\frac{\Gamma_{2}}{\rho_{2}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><mfrac><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><msub><mi>ρ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mfrac></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>W</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><mfrac><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><msub><mi>ρ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mfrac></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H_{1}=Y_{s}-\frac{\Gamma_{1}}{\rho_{1}},H_{2}=W_{s}-\frac{\Gamma_{2}}{\rho_{2}}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="A4.p9" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A4.p9.1" class="ltx_p">The overall LRSC algorithm is listed in Alg. <a href="#alg2" title="Algorithm 2 ‣ Appendix D AODL algorithm and derivation details ‣ Sparse Coding Representation of 2-way Data" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>. We first initialize all variables by sampling from a normal distribution <math id="A4.p9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{N}(0,1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{N}(0,1)</annotation></semantics></math> (Step 3) and then iterate over the derived 0-gradient updates for each variable in turn: update <math id="A4.p9.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Ys" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Ys</annotation></semantics></math> (line 5-9); update <math id="A4.p9.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Ws" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>W</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Ws</annotation></semantics></math> (line 10-14); update <math id="A4.p9.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U_{s},V_{s}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>U</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>s</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U_{s},V_{s}</annotation></semantics></math> (line 15-17); and update <math id="A4.p9.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Gamma_{1},\Gamma_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Gamma_{1},\Gamma_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> in the end. The eigendecomposition steps (line 6 and line 11) are the most expensive steps (cubic in their input) since eigendecomposition compared to matrix multiplications requires iterations (depending on the solver). As a result, eigendecomposition is dominating the running time of each iteration of LRSC with a complexity of <math id="A4.p9.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="O(P^{3}+Q^{3}+k^{3})." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>P</mi><mn>3</mn></msup><mo>+</mo><msup><mi>Q</mi><mn>3</mn></msup><mo>+</mo><msup><mi>k</mi><mn>3</mn></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">O(P^{3}+Q^{3}+k^{3}).</annotation></semantics></math></p>
</div>
<figure id="alg2" class="ltx_float ltx_float_algorithm ltx_framed ltx_framed_top">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_float"><span id="alg2.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Algorithm 2</span> </span> LRSC</figcaption>
<div id="alg2.4" class="ltx_listing ltx_framed ltx_framed_topbottom ltx_listing">
<div id="alg2.l1" class="ltx_listingline">
            <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline">1:</span>
            
            
          <span id="alg2.l1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Input:</span> A single samples <math id="alg2.l1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{s}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{s}</annotation></semantics></math>, dictionaries <math id="alg2.l1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L,R" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>R</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L,R</annotation></semantics></math>, encoding rank <math id="alg2.l1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> and sparsity params. <math id="alg2.l1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda_{1},\lambda_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>λ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>λ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda_{1},\lambda_{2}</annotation></semantics></math>

</div>
<div id="alg2.l2" class="ltx_listingline">
            <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline">2:</span>
            
            
          <span id="alg2.l2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Output:</span> Encodings <math id="alg2.l2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y_{s},W_{s}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>W</mi><mi>s</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y_{s},W_{s}</annotation></semantics></math>

</div>
<div id="alg2.l3" class="ltx_listingline">
            <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline">3:</span>
            
            
          Initialize <math id="alg2.l3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y_{s},W_{s},U_{s},V_{s},\Gamma_{1},\Gamma_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>W</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>U</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y_{s},W_{s},U_{s},V_{s},\Gamma_{1},\Gamma_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> randomly

</div>
<div id="alg2.l4" class="ltx_listingline">
            <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline">4:</span>
            
            
          <span id="alg2.l4.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">repeat</span>
</div>
<div id="alg2.l5" class="ltx_listingline">
            <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline">5:</span>
            
            
            <math id="alg2.l5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B=W_{s}R^{T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>W</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>R</mi><mi>T</mi></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B=W_{s}R^{T}</annotation></semantics></math>

</div>
<div id="alg2.l6" class="ltx_listingline">
            <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline">6:</span>
            
            
            <math id="alg2.l6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q_{1}\Lambda_{1}Q_{1}^{T}=eig(L^{T}L);~~Q_{2}\Lambda_{2}Q_{2}^{T}=eig(BB^{T})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>Q</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>Q</mi><mn>1</mn><mi>T</mi></msubsup></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>L</mi><mi>T</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>L</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.827em">;</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>Q</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>Q</mi><mn>2</mn><mi>T</mi></msubsup></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>B</mi><mi>T</mi></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q_{1}\Lambda_{1}Q_{1}^{T}=eig(L^{T}L);~~Q_{2}\Lambda_{2}Q_{2}^{T}=eig(BB^{T})</annotation></semantics></math>

</div>
<div id="alg2.l7" class="ltx_listingline">
            <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline">7:</span>
            
            
            <math id="alg2.l7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Pi_{1}=2L^{T}X_{s}B^{T}+\rho_{1}U_{s}+\Gamma_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Π</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>L</mi><mi>T</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>B</mi><mi>T</mi></msup></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>ρ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>U</mi><mi>s</mi></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Pi_{1}=2L^{T}X_{s}B^{T}+\rho_{1}U_{s}+\Gamma_{1}</annotation></semantics></math>

</div>
<div id="alg2.l8" class="ltx_listingline">
            <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline">8:</span>
            
            
            <math id="alg2.l8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{[E_{1}]}_{i,j}=\frac{{[Q_{1}^{T}\Pi_{1}Q_{2}]}_{i,j}}{2{[\Lambda_{1}]}_{i,i}{[\Lambda_{2}]}_{j,j}+\rho_{1}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msub><mi>E</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mfrac><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>Q</mi><mn>1</mn><mi>T</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Π</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>Q</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>ρ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></mfrac></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{[E_{1}]}_{i,j}=\frac{{[Q_{1}^{T}\Pi_{1}Q_{2}]}_{i,j}}{2{[\Lambda_{1}]}_{i,i}{[\Lambda_{2}]}_{j,j}+\rho_{1}}</annotation></semantics></math>

</div>
<div id="alg2.l9" class="ltx_listingline">
            <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline">9:</span>
            
            
            <math id="alg2.l9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y_{s}=Q_{1}E_{1}Q_{2}^{T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>Q</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>E</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>Q</mi><mn>2</mn><mi>T</mi></msubsup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y_{s}=Q_{1}E_{1}Q_{2}^{T}</annotation></semantics></math>

</div>
<div id="alg2.l10" class="ltx_listingline">
            <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline">10:</span>
            
            
            <math id="alg2.l10.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A=LY_{s}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>s</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A=LY_{s}</annotation></semantics></math>

</div>
<div id="alg2.l11" class="ltx_listingline">
            <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline">11:</span>
            
            
            <math id="alg2.l11.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q_{3}\Lambda_{3}Q_{3}^{T}=eig(A^{T}A);~~Q_{4}\Lambda_{4}Q_{4}^{T}=eig(R^{T}R)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>Q</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>Q</mi><mn>3</mn><mi>T</mi></msubsup></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>A</mi><mi>T</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>A</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.827em">;</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>Q</mi><mn>4</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi><mn>4</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>Q</mi><mn>4</mn><mi>T</mi></msubsup></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>R</mi><mi>T</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>R</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q_{3}\Lambda_{3}Q_{3}^{T}=eig(A^{T}A);~~Q_{4}\Lambda_{4}Q_{4}^{T}=eig(R^{T}R)</annotation></semantics></math>

</div>
<div id="alg2.l12" class="ltx_listingline">
            <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline">12:</span>
            
            
            <math id="alg2.l12.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Pi_{2}=2A^{T}X_{s}R+\rho_{2}V_{s}+\Gamma_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Π</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>A</mi><mi>T</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>R</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>ρ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>s</mi></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Pi_{2}=2A^{T}X_{s}R+\rho_{2}V_{s}+\Gamma_{2}</annotation></semantics></math>

</div>
<div id="alg2.l13" class="ltx_listingline">
            <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline">13:</span>
            
            
            <math id="alg2.l13.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{[E_{2}]}_{i,j}=\frac{{[Q_{3}^{T}\Pi_{3}Q_{4}]}_{i,j}}{2{[\Lambda_{4}]}_{i,i}{[\Lambda_{3}]}_{j,j}+\rho_{2}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msub><mi>E</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mfrac><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>Q</mi><mn>3</mn><mi>T</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Π</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>Q</mi><mn>4</mn></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi><mn>4</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Λ</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>ρ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></mfrac></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{[E_{2}]}_{i,j}=\frac{{[Q_{3}^{T}\Pi_{3}Q_{4}]}_{i,j}}{2{[\Lambda_{4}]}_{i,i}{[\Lambda_{3}]}_{j,j}+\rho_{2}}</annotation></semantics></math>

</div>
<div id="alg2.l14" class="ltx_listingline">
            <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline">14:</span>
            
            
            <math id="alg2.l14.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W_{s}=Q_{3}E_{2}Q_{4}^{T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>W</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>Q</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>E</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>Q</mi><mn>4</mn><mi>T</mi></msubsup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W_{s}=Q_{3}E_{2}Q_{4}^{T}</annotation></semantics></math>

</div>
<div id="alg2.l15" class="ltx_listingline">
            <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline">15:</span>
            
            
            <math id="alg2.l15.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H_{1}=Y_{s}-\frac{\Gamma_{1}}{\rho_{1}},H_{2}=W_{s}-\frac{\Gamma_{2}}{\rho_{2}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><mfrac><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><msub><mi>ρ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mfrac></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>W</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><mfrac><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><msub><mi>ρ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mfrac></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H_{1}=Y_{s}-\frac{\Gamma_{1}}{\rho_{1}},H_{2}=W_{s}-\frac{\Gamma_{2}}{\rho_{2}}</annotation></semantics></math>

</div>
<div id="alg2.l16" class="ltx_listingline">
            <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline">16:</span>
            
            
            <math id="alg2.l16.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{[U_{s}]}_{i,j}=\text{sign}({[H_{1}]}_{i,j})\times\max{(|{[H_{1}]}_{i,j}|-\frac{\lambda_{1}}{\rho_{1}},0)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msub><mi>U</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mtext>sign</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mrow><mi>max</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>−</mo><mfrac><msub><mi>λ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><msub><mi>ρ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mfrac></mrow><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{[U_{s}]}_{i,j}=\text{sign}({[H_{1}]}_{i,j})\times\max{(|{[H_{1}]}_{i,j}|-\frac{\lambda_{1}}{\rho_{1}},0)}</annotation></semantics></math>

</div>
<div id="alg2.l17" class="ltx_listingline">
            <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline">17:</span>
            
            
            <math id="alg2.l17.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{[V_{s}]}_{i,j}=\text{sign}({[H_{2}]}_{i,j})\times\max{(|{[H_{2}]}_{i,j}|-\frac{\lambda_{2}}{\rho_{2}},0)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mtext>sign</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mrow><mi>max</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>−</mo><mfrac><msub><mi>λ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><msub><mi>ρ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mfrac></mrow><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{[V_{s}]}_{i,j}=\text{sign}({[H_{2}]}_{i,j})\times\max{(|{[H_{2}]}_{i,j}|-\frac{\lambda_{2}}{\rho_{2}},0)}</annotation></semantics></math>

</div>
<div id="alg2.l18" class="ltx_listingline">
            <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline">18:</span>
            
            
            <math id="alg2.l18.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Gamma_{1}=\Gamma_{1}+\rho_{1}(U_{s}-Y_{s})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>ρ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>U</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>s</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Gamma_{1}=\Gamma_{1}+\rho_{1}(U_{s}-Y_{s})</annotation></semantics></math>

</div>
<div id="alg2.l19" class="ltx_listingline">
            <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline">19:</span>
            
            
            <math id="alg2.l19.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Gamma_{2}=\Gamma_{2}+\rho_{2}(V_{s}-W_{s})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>ρ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>W</mi><mi>s</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Gamma_{2}=\Gamma_{2}+\rho_{2}(V_{s}-W_{s})</annotation></semantics></math>

</div>
<div id="alg2.l20" class="ltx_listingline">
            <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline">20:</span>
            
            
          <span id="alg2.l20.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">until</span> Convergence or fixed max iterations

</div>
</div>
</figure>
<div id="A4.p10" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A4.p10.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="A4.p10.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Stage II: Dictionary updates.</span> We employ gradient projection for dictionary updates given fixed <math id="A4.p10.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y_{s},W_{s},\forall s\leq S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>W</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">∀</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y_{s},W_{s},\forall s\leq S</annotation></semantics></math> with objective:</p>
<table id="A4.E52" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_table">
<tbody>
<tr id="A4.E52X" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A4.E52X.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\underset{L,R}{\mathrm{argmin}}\sum_{s=1}^{S}(" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><munder accentunder="true"><mi>argmin</mi><mrow><mi mathsize="0.700em">L</mi><mo mathsize="0.700em">,</mo><mi mathsize="0.700em">R</mi></mrow></munder><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>S</mi></munderover></mstyle><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo fence="true" lspace="0em">CLOSE</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\underset{L,R}{\mathrm{argmin}}\sum_{s=1}^{S}(</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A4.E52X.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle||X_{s}-LY_{s}W_{s}R^{T}||_{F}^{2}," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>W</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>R</mi><mi>T</mi></msup></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mi>F</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle||X_{s}-LY_{s}W_{s}R^{T}||_{F}^{2},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equationgroup ltx_align_right">(52)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A4.p10.3" class="ltx_p">and by setting setting gradients w.r.t. <math id="A4.p10.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>L</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L</annotation></semantics></math> to zero, we obtain:</p>
<table id="A4.E53" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_table">
<tbody>
<tr id="A4.E53X" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A4.E53X.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\sum_{s=1}^{S}-2(X_{s}-LY_{s}W_{s}R^{T})RW_{s}^{T}Y_{s}^{T}=0," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo></mstyle><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><mi>S</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><mi>L</mi><mi>Y</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>s</mi></msub><mi>W</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>s</mi></msub><mi>R</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mi>T</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mi>R</mi><mi>W</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>s</mi></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><mi>T</mi></msup><mi>Y</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>s</mi></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><mi>T</mi></msup><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\sum_{s=1}^{S}-2(X_{s}-LY_{s}W_{s}R^{T})RW_{s}^{T}Y_{s}^{T}=0,</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equationgroup ltx_align_right">(53)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A4.p10.4" class="ltx_p">with a closed-form solution for <math id="A4.p10.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>L</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L</annotation></semantics></math>:</p>
<table id="A4.E54" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_table">
<tbody>
<tr id="A4.E54X" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A4.E54X.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle L=(\sum X_{s}RW_{s}^{T}Y_{s}^{T})(\sum Y_{s}W_{s}R^{T}RW_{s}^{T}Y_{s}^{T})^{-1}." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mrow><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>R</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>W</mi><mi>s</mi><mi>T</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>Y</mi><mi>s</mi><mi>T</mi></msubsup></mrow></mrow></mstyle><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mrow><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>W</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>R</mi><mi>T</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>R</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>W</mi><mi>s</mi><mi>T</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>Y</mi><mi>s</mi><mi>T</mi></msubsup></mrow></mrow></mstyle><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle L=(\sum X_{s}RW_{s}^{T}Y_{s}^{T})(\sum Y_{s}W_{s}R^{T}RW_{s}^{T}Y_{s}^{T})^{-1}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equationgroup ltx_align_right">(54)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A4.p10.5" class="ltx_p"><math id="A4.p10.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>R</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R</annotation></semantics></math>’s update is derived in a similar manner and is also listed in Alg. <a href="#alg1" title="Algorithm 1 ‣ 5 AODL: Dictionary learning for low-rank sparse coding ‣ Sparse Coding Representation of 2-way Data" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>
Atoms of both dictionaries are normalized by <em id="A4.p10.5.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">normalize(<math id="A4.p10.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\cdot" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>⋅</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\cdot</annotation></semantics></math>)</em> in Alg. <a href="#alg1" title="Algorithm 1 ‣ 5 AODL: Dictionary learning for low-rank sparse coding ‣ Sparse Coding Representation of 2-way Data" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> so the magnitude in representing samples is fully represented in the coding matrices as opposed to the dictionaries.</p>
</div>
<div id="A4.p11" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A4.p11.1" class="ltx_p">The dictionary updates involve matrix multiplications and matrix inversions, which run in cubic time in the input size. However, matrix inversion is in general much slower than regular multiplication. Thus, the matrix inversion term is dominating the running time with a complexity of <math id="A4.p11.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="O(T+P^{3}+Q^{3})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>+</mo><msup><mi>P</mi><mn>3</mn></msup><mo>+</mo><msup><mi>Q</mi><mn>3</mn></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">O(T+P^{3}+Q^{3})</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="A4.p11.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math> is the product of the maximum <math id="A4.p11.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>3</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">3</annotation></semantics></math> values among <math id="A4.p11.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{N,M,P,Q,k\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>P</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>Q</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{N,M,P,Q,k\}</annotation></semantics></math>. <math id="A4.p11.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math> represents the run time of matrix multiplication inside the inversion term, and <math id="A4.p11.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P^{3}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>P</mi><mn>3</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P^{3}</annotation></semantics></math> is the inversion in the solution of <math id="A4.p11.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>L</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="A4.p11.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q^{3}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>Q</mi><mn>3</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q^{3}</annotation></semantics></math> is the inversion in the solution of <math id="A4.p11.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>R</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A5" class="ltx_appendix">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_appendix">Appendix E </span>CMOD-ADMM derivation</h2>

<div id="A5.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A5.p1.2" class="ltx_p">While the original paper introducing the CMOD method employs 2D-OMP for the sparse coding step, our experimental analysis (Tbl. <a href="#S6.T1" title="Table 1 ‣ 6 Experimental evaluation ‣ Sparse Coding Representation of 2-way Data" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>) demonstrated that the reliance on OMP limits the method’s scalability as NNZ grows. For this reason we derive and employ a version of CMOD with ADMM sparse coding in the sparse coding subproblem (with fixed dictionaries <math id="A5.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>L</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A5.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>R</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R</annotation></semantics></math>) is as follows:</p>
<table id="A5.E55" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_table">
<tbody>
<tr id="A5.E55X" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A5.E55X.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\underset{Z}{\mathrm{argmin}}\sum_{s=1}^{S}(" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><munder accentunder="true"><mi>argmin</mi><mo mathsize="0.700em">𝑍</mo></munder><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>S</mi></munderover></mstyle><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo fence="true" lspace="0em">CLOSE</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\underset{Z}{\mathrm{argmin}}\sum_{s=1}^{S}(</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A5.E55X.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle||X_{s}-LZ_{s}R^{T}||_{F}^{2}+\lambda_{1}||Z_{s}||_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>Z</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>R</mi><mi>T</mi></msup></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mi>F</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>λ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><msub><mi>Z</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle||X_{s}-LZ_{s}R^{T}||_{F}^{2}+\lambda_{1}||Z_{s}||_{1}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equationgroup ltx_align_right">(55)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="A5.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A5.p2.1" class="ltx_p">To obtain an ADMM solution we introduce a proxy variable <math id="A5.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U_{s}=Zs" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>U</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>Z</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U_{s}=Zs</annotation></semantics></math> and obtain the following Lagrangian form for sample <math id="A5.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>s</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s</annotation></semantics></math>:</p>
</div>
<div id="A5.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<table id="A5.E56" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_table">
<tbody>
<tr id="A5.E56X" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A5.E56X.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\mathcal{L}(Z_{s},U_{s})=||X_{s}-LZ_{s}R^{T}||_{F}^{2}+\lambda_{1}||U_{s}||_{1}+\frac{\rho}{2}||U_{s}-Z_{s}+\frac{\Gamma}{\rho}||_{F}^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>Z</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo mathsize="0.700em">,</mo><msub><mi>U</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><msubsup><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><msubsup><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>Z</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>R</mi><mi>T</mi></msup></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mi>F</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>λ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><msub><mi>U</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mi>ρ</mi><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mstyle></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>U</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>Z</mi><mi>s</mi></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mi>ρ</mi></mfrac></mstyle></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mi>F</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mathcal{L}(Z_{s},U_{s})=||X_{s}-LZ_{s}R^{T}||_{F}^{2}+\lambda_{1}||U_{s}||_{1}+\frac{\rho}{2}||U_{s}-Z_{s}+\frac{\Gamma}{\rho}||_{F}^{2}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equationgroup ltx_align_right">(56)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="A5.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A5.p4.1" class="ltx_p">The above equation is similar to Eq. <a href="#A4.E46" title="In Appendix D AODL algorithm and derivation details ‣ Sparse Coding Representation of 2-way Data" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">46</span></a>. By simply replacing <math id="A5.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>B</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="A5.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>R</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="A5.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y_{s}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y_{s}</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="A5.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z_{s}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>Z</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z_{s}</annotation></semantics></math>, we can obtain closed-form updates for <math id="A5.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z_{s}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>Z</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z_{s}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A5.p4.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U_{S}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>U</mi><mi>S</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U_{S}</annotation></semantics></math> using the same steps.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A6" class="ltx_appendix">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_appendix">Appendix F </span><span id="A6.2" class="ltx_text ltx_markedasmath">AODL</span> (and CMOD) with missing values</h2>

<div id="A6.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A6.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A6.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_markedasmath ltx_font_bold">AODL</span><span id="A6.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> with missing values.</span> The missing values objective for our problems is:</p>
</div>
<div id="A6.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<table id="A6.E57" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_table">
<tbody>
<tr id="A6.E57X" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A6.E57X.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\underset{L,R,Y,W}{\mathrm{argmin}}\sum_{s=1}^{S}(" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><munder accentunder="true"><mi>argmin</mi><mrow><mi mathsize="0.700em">L</mi><mo mathsize="0.700em">,</mo><mi mathsize="0.700em">R</mi><mo mathsize="0.700em">,</mo><mi mathsize="0.700em">Y</mi><mo mathsize="0.700em">,</mo><mi mathsize="0.700em">W</mi></mrow></munder><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>S</mi></munderover></mstyle><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo fence="true" lspace="0em">CLOSE</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\underset{L,R,Y,W}{\mathrm{argmin}}\sum_{s=1}^{S}(</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A6.E57X.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle||\Omega_{s}\odot(X_{s}-LY_{s}W_{s}R^{T})||_{F}^{2}+\lambda_{1}||Y_{s}||_{1}+\lambda_{2}||W_{s}||_{1})," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo fence="true" rspace="0em">OPEN</mo><mrow><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊙</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>W</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>R</mi><mi>T</mi></msup></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mi>F</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>λ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>λ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><msub><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi>W</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle||\Omega_{s}\odot(X_{s}-LY_{s}W_{s}R^{T})||_{F}^{2}+\lambda_{1}||Y_{s}||_{1}+\lambda_{2}||W_{s}||_{1}),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equationgroup ltx_align_right">(57)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A6.p2.3" class="ltx_p">where <math id="A6.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Omega_{s}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Omega_{s}</annotation></semantics></math> is a sample-specific missing value <math id="A6.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1-0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1-0</annotation></semantics></math> mask and <math id="A6.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\odot" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>⊙</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\odot</annotation></semantics></math> denotes the element-wise product.
To optimize the missing values objective from Eq. <a href="#A6.E57" title="In Appendix F AODL (and CMOD) with missing values ‣ Sparse Coding Representation of 2-way Data" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">57</span></a> we introduce additional proxy variables <math id="A6.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D_{s}=X_{s},U_{s}=Y_{s},V_{s}=W_{s}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>D</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>s</mi></msub></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>U</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>s</mi></msub></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>W</mi><mi>s</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D_{s}=X_{s},U_{s}=Y_{s},V_{s}=W_{s}</annotation></semantics></math>, arriving at the following objective:</p>
<table id="A6.E58" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_table">
<tbody>
<tr id="A6.E58X" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A6.E58X.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\underset{L,R,Y,W}{\mathrm{argmin}}\sum_{s=1}^{S}(" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><munder accentunder="true"><mi>argmin</mi><mrow><mi mathsize="0.700em">L</mi><mo mathsize="0.700em">,</mo><mi mathsize="0.700em">R</mi><mo mathsize="0.700em">,</mo><mi mathsize="0.700em">Y</mi><mo mathsize="0.700em">,</mo><mi mathsize="0.700em">W</mi></mrow></munder><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>S</mi></munderover></mstyle><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo fence="true" lspace="0em">CLOSE</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\underset{L,R,Y,W}{\mathrm{argmin}}\sum_{s=1}^{S}(</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A6.E58X.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle||D_{s}-LY_{s}W_{s}R^{T}||_{F}^{2}+\lambda_{1}||U_{s}||_{1}+\lambda_{2}||V_{s}||_{1}+\lambda_{3}||\Omega_{s}\odot(D_{s}-X_{s})||_{F}^{2})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo fence="true" rspace="0em">OPEN</mo><mrow><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><msub><mi>D</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>W</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>R</mi><mi>T</mi></msup></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mi>F</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo>+</mo><msubsup><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>λ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><msub><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi>U</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>λ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>λ</mi><mn>3</mn></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊙</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>D</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>s</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mi>F</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle||D_{s}-LY_{s}W_{s}R^{T}||_{F}^{2}+\lambda_{1}||U_{s}||_{1}+\lambda_{2}||V_{s}||_{1}+\lambda_{3}||\Omega_{s}\odot(D_{s}-X_{s})||_{F}^{2})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="2" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equationgroup ltx_align_right">(58)</span></td></tr>
<tr id="A6.E58Xa" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A6.E58Xa.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle s.t.~~D_{s}=X_{s}," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">.</mo><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.827em">.</mo><mrow><msub><mi>D</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>s</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle s.t.~~D_{s}=X_{s},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A6.E58Xa.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle Y_{s}=U_{s},W_{s}=V_{s}," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Y</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mi>U</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>W</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mi>V</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle Y_{s}=U_{s},W_{s}=V_{s},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="A6.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A6.p3.1" class="ltx_p">We form the corresponding Lagrangian function for sample <math id="A6.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>s</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s</annotation></semantics></math>:</p>
</div>
<div id="A6.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<table id="A6.E59" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_table">
<tbody>
<tr id="A6.E59X" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A6.E59X.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\mathcal{L}(D_{s},Y_{s},W_{s},U_{s},V_{s})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>D</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>W</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>U</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mathcal{L}(D_{s},Y_{s},W_{s},U_{s},V_{s})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="3" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equationgroup ltx_align_right">(59)</span></td></tr>
<tr id="A6.E59Xa" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A6.E59Xa.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>=</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A6.E59Xa.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle||D_{s}-LY_{s}W_{s}R^{T}||_{F}^{2}+\lambda_{1}||U_{s}||_{1}+\lambda_{2}||V_{s}||_{1}+\lambda_{3}||\Omega_{s}\odot(D_{s}-X_{s})||_{F}^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><msub><mi>D</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>W</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>R</mi><mi>T</mi></msup></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mi>F</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>λ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><msub><mi>U</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo><msubsup><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>λ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><msub><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>λ</mi><mn>3</mn></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊙</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>D</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>s</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mi>F</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle||D_{s}-LY_{s}W_{s}R^{T}||_{F}^{2}+\lambda_{1}||U_{s}||_{1}+\lambda_{2}||V_{s}||_{1}+\lambda_{3}||\Omega_{s}\odot(D_{s}-X_{s})||_{F}^{2}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr>
<tr id="A6.E59Xb" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A6.E59Xb.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle+\frac{\rho_{1}}{2}||U_{s}-Y_{s}+\frac{\Gamma_{1}}{\rho_{1}}||_{F}^{2}+\frac{\rho_{2}}{2}||V_{s}-W_{s}+\frac{\Gamma_{2}}{\rho_{2}}||_{F}^{2}." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><msub><mi>ρ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>U</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>s</mi></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><msub><mi>ρ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mfrac></mstyle></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mi>F</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><msub><mi>ρ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>W</mi><mi>s</mi></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><msub><mi>ρ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mfrac></mstyle></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mi>F</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle+\frac{\rho_{1}}{2}||U_{s}-Y_{s}+\frac{\Gamma_{1}}{\rho_{1}}||_{F}^{2}+\frac{\rho_{2}}{2}||V_{s}-W_{s}+\frac{\Gamma_{2}}{\rho_{2}}||_{F}^{2}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="A6.p5" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A6.p5.2" class="ltx_p">To update <math id="A6.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D_{s}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>D</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D_{s}</annotation></semantics></math>, we have the following optimization problem:</p>
<table id="A6.E60" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_table">
<tbody>
<tr id="A6.E60X" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A6.E60X.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\underset{D_{s}}{\mathrm{argmin}}||D_{s}-LY_{s}W_{s}R^{T}||_{F}^{2}+\lambda_{3}||\Omega_{s}\odot(D_{s}-X_{s})||_{F}^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><munder accentunder="true"><mi>argmin</mi><msub><mi mathsize="0.700em">D</mi><mi mathsize="0.710em">s</mi></msub></munder><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><msub><mi>D</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>W</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>R</mi><mi>T</mi></msup></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mi>F</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>λ</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊙</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>D</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>s</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mi>F</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\underset{D_{s}}{\mathrm{argmin}}||D_{s}-LY_{s}W_{s}R^{T}||_{F}^{2}+\lambda_{3}||\Omega_{s}\odot(D_{s}-X_{s})||_{F}^{2}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equationgroup ltx_align_right">(60)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="A6.p6" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A6.p6.1" class="ltx_p">Taking the gradient and setting the equation to zero, we can solve <math id="A6.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D_{s}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>D</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D_{s}</annotation></semantics></math>:</p>
</div>
<div id="A6.p7" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<table id="A6.E61" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_table">
<tbody>
<tr id="A6.E61X" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A6.E61X.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle Ds=(LY_{s}W_{s}R^{T}+\lambda_{3}\Omega_{s}\odot X_{s})\oslash(I+\lambda_{3}\Omega_{s})," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>W</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>R</mi><mi>T</mi></msup></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>λ</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mi>s</mi></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊙</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>s</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>⊘</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>λ</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mi>s</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle Ds=(LY_{s}W_{s}R^{T}+\lambda_{3}\Omega_{s}\odot X_{s})\oslash(I+\lambda_{3}\Omega_{s}),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equationgroup ltx_align_right">(61)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A6.p7.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="A6.p7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\oslash" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>⊘</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\oslash</annotation></semantics></math> is element-wise division.</p>
</div>
<div id="A6.p8" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A6.p8.1" class="ltx_p">The update for other variables <math id="A6.p8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y_{s},W_{s},U_{s},V_{s}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>W</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>U</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>s</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y_{s},W_{s},U_{s},V_{s}</annotation></semantics></math> are exactly the same as in Alg. <a href="#alg2" title="Algorithm 2 ‣ Appendix D AODL algorithm and derivation details ‣ Sparse Coding Representation of 2-way Data" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>, we will omit here.</p>
</div>
<div id="A6.p9" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A6.p9.2" class="ltx_p">To update the dictionaries, we have the following objective function which is slightly different with the one in the main paper:</p>
<table id="A6.E62" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_table">
<tbody>
<tr id="A6.E62X" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A6.E62X.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\underset{L,R}{\mathrm{argmin}}\sum_{s=1}^{S}(" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><munder accentunder="true"><mi>argmin</mi><mrow><mi mathsize="0.700em">L</mi><mo mathsize="0.700em">,</mo><mi mathsize="0.700em">R</mi></mrow></munder><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>S</mi></munderover></mstyle><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo fence="true" lspace="0em">CLOSE</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\underset{L,R}{\mathrm{argmin}}\sum_{s=1}^{S}(</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A6.E62X.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle||D_{s}-LY_{s}W_{s}R^{T}||_{F}^{2}," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><msub><mi>D</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>W</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>R</mi><mi>T</mi></msup></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mi>F</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle||D_{s}-LY_{s}W_{s}R^{T}||_{F}^{2},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equationgroup ltx_align_right">(62)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A6.p9.3" class="ltx_p">and by setting <math id="A6.p9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\partial f/\partial L=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo>∂</mo><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo rspace="0.055em">/</mo><mrow><mo>∂</mo><mi>L</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\partial f/\partial L=0</annotation></semantics></math>, we get a closed-form solution for <math id="A6.p9.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>L</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L</annotation></semantics></math>:</p>
<table id="A6.E63" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_table">
<tbody>
<tr id="A6.E63X" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A6.E63X.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle L=(\sum D_{s}RW_{s}^{T}Y_{s}^{T})(\sum Y_{s}W_{s}R^{T}RW_{s}^{T}Y_{s}^{T})^{-1}," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mrow><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><msub><mi>D</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>R</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>W</mi><mi>s</mi><mi>T</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>Y</mi><mi>s</mi><mi>T</mi></msubsup></mrow></mrow></mstyle><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mrow><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>W</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>R</mi><mi>T</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>R</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>W</mi><mi>s</mi><mi>T</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>Y</mi><mi>s</mi><mi>T</mi></msubsup></mrow></mrow></mstyle><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle L=(\sum D_{s}RW_{s}^{T}Y_{s}^{T})(\sum Y_{s}W_{s}R^{T}RW_{s}^{T}Y_{s}^{T})^{-1},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equationgroup ltx_align_right">(63)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A6.p9.4" class="ltx_p">and <math id="A6.p9.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>R</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R</annotation></semantics></math> could be solved similarly. Both dictionaries are normalized to unit atom length.</p>
</div>
<div id="A6.p10" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A6.p10.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="A6.p10.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">CMOD with missing values.</span> Note that the sparse coding stage of CMOD-ADMM is similar to our objective with the key difference of a single sample-wise encoding matrix <math id="A6.p10.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z_{s}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>Z</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z_{s}</annotation></semantics></math>. To derive a missing-value-aware version of CMOD-ADMM we employ the same ADMM approach as the one outlined above for <span id="A6.p10.2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_markedasmath">AODL</span>, with proxy variables for <math id="A6.p10.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z_{s}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>Z</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z_{s}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A6.p10.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{s}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{s}</annotation></semantics></math> only:</p>
<table id="A6.E64" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_table">
<tbody>
<tr id="A6.E64X" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A6.E64X.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\underset{Z}{\mathrm{argmin}}\sum_{s=1}^{S}(" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><munder accentunder="true"><mi>argmin</mi><mo mathsize="0.700em">𝑍</mo></munder><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>S</mi></munderover></mstyle><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo fence="true" lspace="0em">CLOSE</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\underset{Z}{\mathrm{argmin}}\sum_{s=1}^{S}(</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A6.E64X.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle||D_{s}-LZ_{s}R^{T}||_{F}^{2}+\lambda_{1}||U_{s}||_{1}+\lambda_{2}||\Omega_{s}\odot(D_{s}-X_{s})||_{F}^{2})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo fence="true" rspace="0em">OPEN</mo><mrow><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><msub><mi>D</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>Z</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>R</mi><mi>T</mi></msup></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mi>F</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>λ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><msub><mi>U</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>λ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Ω</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊙</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>D</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>s</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mi>F</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle||D_{s}-LZ_{s}R^{T}||_{F}^{2}+\lambda_{1}||U_{s}||_{1}+\lambda_{2}||\Omega_{s}\odot(D_{s}-X_{s})||_{F}^{2})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="2" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equationgroup ltx_align_right">(64)</span></td></tr>
<tr id="A6.E64Xa" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A6.E64Xa.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle s.t.~~D_{s}=X_{s}," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">.</mo><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.827em">.</mo><mrow><msub><mi>D</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>s</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle s.t.~~D_{s}=X_{s},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A6.E64Xa.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle Z_{s}=U_{s}." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>Z</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>U</mi><mi>s</mi></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle Z_{s}=U_{s}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A6.p10.3" class="ltx_p">Updates for the above objective when the dictionaries are fixed are obtained in the same manner as those for <span id="A6.p10.3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_markedasmath">AODL</span>. For the dictionary update stage, again, we just need to replace <math id="A6.p10.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{s}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{s}</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="A6.p10.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D_{s}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>D</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D_{s}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A7" class="ltx_appendix">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_appendix">Appendix G </span>Dataset description</h2>

<div id="A7.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A7.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We next provide more context on the dataset preparation in which we followed the protocol for preparation from prior work that employed the same datasets.</p>
</div>
<div id="A7.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A7.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A7.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Synthetic data.</span> We generate synthetic data according to the model <math id="A7.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{s}=LY_{s}W_{s}R^{T}+\epsilon" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>W</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>R</mi><mi>T</mi></msup></mrow><mo>+</mo><mi>ϵ</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{s}=LY_{s}W_{s}R^{T}+\epsilon</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="A7.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\epsilon" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ϵ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\epsilon</annotation></semantics></math> is Gaussian noise. Dictionaries <math id="A7.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L\in\mathbb{R}^{20\times 20}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mn>20</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mn>20</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L\in\mathbb{R}^{20\times 20}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A7.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R\in\mathbb{R}^{30\times 30}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mn>30</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mn>30</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R\in\mathbb{R}^{30\times 30}</annotation></semantics></math> contain random unit-norm atoms and encodings are of rank <math id="A7.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k=3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k=3</annotation></semantics></math>, i.e., <math id="A7.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y\in\mathbb{R}^{20\times 3},W\in\mathbb{R}^{3\times 30}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mn>20</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>W</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mn>30</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y\in\mathbb{R}^{20\times 3},W\in\mathbb{R}^{3\times 30}</annotation></semantics></math>. For each sample <math id="A7.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y_{s}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y_{s}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A7.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W_{s}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>W</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W_{s}</annotation></semantics></math> each contains <math id="A7.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="15" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>15</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">15</annotation></semantics></math> randomly selected coefficients with normally distributed in <math id="A7.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{N}(0,1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{N}(0,1)</annotation></semantics></math>. There are a total of <math id="A7.p2.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="100" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>100</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">100</annotation></semantics></math> training samples which are used to learn the dictionaries.</p>
</div>
<div id="A7.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A7.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A7.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Real-world datasets.</span> We employ <math id="A7.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="5" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>5</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">5</annotation></semantics></math> real-world datasets with temporal and spatial dimensions to be able to evaluate against competing techniques like TGSD <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib27" title="" class="ltx_ref">27</a>]</cite> employing analytical temporal and graph dictionaries. To prepare the datasets we follow the same protocols as prior work. The datasets span multiple domains: content exchange (<em id="A7.p3.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Twitch</em> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib31" title="" class="ltx_ref">31</a>]</cite>), web traffic (<em id="A7.p3.1.3" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Wiki</em> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">1</a>]</cite>), sensor network readings (<em id="A7.p3.1.4" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Road</em> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">6</a>]</cite> and <em id="A7.p3.1.5" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Air</em> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib37" title="" class="ltx_ref">37</a>]</cite>) and a social interaction (<em id="A7.p3.1.6" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">MIT</em> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">11</a>]</cite>). In order to create multiple samples for dictionary learning, we slice the data on the larger of its two dimensions (time or spatial/graph extent). We also consider alternative slicing (see Fig. <a href="#A9.F7.sf2" title="In Figure 7 ‣ Appendix I Additional experiments ‣ Sparse Coding Representation of 2-way Data" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7(b)</span></a>) that confirms our comparative analysis findings.</p>
</div>
<div id="A7.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A7.p4.1" class="ltx_p">Twitch <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib31" title="" class="ltx_ref">31</a>]</cite> contains viewer-streamer temporal interactions. We create a graph among viewers and add an edge between a pair of viewers if they viewed the same stream at least 3 times. We use the largest connected component of the co-viewing graph. Values in data samples <math id="A7.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X\in\mathbb{R}^{9000\times 512}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mn>9000</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mn>512</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X\in\mathbb{R}^{9000\times 512}</annotation></semantics></math> represent the number of minutes in any given hour that a viewer spent viewing any streams (i.e., their level of activity). We slice the data randomly into <math id="A7.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="30" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>30</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">30</annotation></semantics></math> samples along the graph dimension.</p>
</div>
<div id="A7.p5" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A7.p5.1" class="ltx_p">The Wiki dataset <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">1</a>]</cite> records hourly number of views of Wikipedia articles over <math id="A7.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="792" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>792</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">792</annotation></semantics></math> hours. A co-click graph among articles is constructed by placing edges between articles with at least <math id="A7.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="10" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>10</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">10</annotation></semantics></math> pairwise click events (clicked by the same IPs) within a day. A breadth-first-search (snowball) subgraph of <math id="A7.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="11400" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>11400</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">11400</annotation></semantics></math> around the China article is selected and then sliced into <math id="A7.p5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="38" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>38</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">38</annotation></semantics></math> samples along the graph dimension.</p>
</div>
<div id="A7.p6" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A7.p6.1" class="ltx_p">The Road <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">6</a>]</cite> dataset consists of <math id="A7.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2780" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>2780</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2780</annotation></semantics></math> highway speed sensors in the LA area. We use the average speed for <math id="A7.p6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="30" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>30</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">30</annotation></semantics></math> days at <math id="A7.p6.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="5" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>5</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">5</annotation></semantics></math>-minute interval (<math id="A7.p6.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="8640" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>8640</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">8640</annotation></semantics></math> timesteps) as our signal matrix. The graph is based on connected road segments. We slice the data into 30 samples on the time dimension.</p>
</div>
<div id="A7.p7" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A7.p7.1" class="ltx_p">MIT <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">11</a>]</cite> is a communication dataset of timestamped messages between users and a weighted social graph. We split the data along its time dimension.</p>
</div>
<div id="A7.p8" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A7.p8.1" class="ltx_p">The Air <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib37" title="" class="ltx_ref">37</a>]</cite> dataset contains the the number of flights between connected airports (nodes), while the edges connect flight origin and destination. Temporal snapshots represent the number of incoming flights over a 6 hour window. We remove small airports will less than <math id="A7.p8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="8" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>8</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">8</annotation></semantics></math> flights a day and split the data along its graph dimension obtaining 25 samples.</p>
</div>
<div id="A7.p9" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A7.p9.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A7.p9.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">A note on the selection of real-world datasets.</span> We employ the above spatio(graph)-temporal (ST) data as they have been shown to align to low-rank encoding models due to clustered (shared) behavior present in the temporal and spatial mode observed in the TGSD <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib27" title="" class="ltx_ref">27</a>]</cite> baseline. Different from the baseline, however, our method AODL does not use graph or temporal information associated with the data but learns temporal and spatial dictionaries from scratch. The learned dictionaries perform better than analytical ones employed by TGSD <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib27" title="" class="ltx_ref">27</a>]</cite> as demonstrated in in our comparative analysis in the main paper. The ST datasets also allow us to perform qualitative analysis (case studies) and also create controlled synthetic data from GT dictionaries akin to the setup in TGSD.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A8" class="ltx_appendix">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_appendix">Appendix H </span>Baselines and metrics of success</h2>

<div id="A8.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A8.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In this section we provide additional description and justification for the selected baselines.</p>
</div>
<div id="A8.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A8.p2.1" class="ltx_p">TGSD employs analytical dictionaries but has a low-rank model for the encoding similar to <span id="A8.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_markedasmath">AODL</span>. Within this baseline we employ the authors’ implementation and the GFT dictionary based on data graphs for graph dimensions and the Ramanujan periodic dictionary for the time dimensions.</p>
</div>
<div id="A8.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A8.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Shahriari-Mehr et Al. <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib34" title="" class="ltx_ref">34</a>]</cite> proposed two methods 2D-CMOD and 2D-MOD for 2D dictionary learning among which 2D-CMOD converged faster to a better solution according to the authors’ experiments. Hence we adopt it as a baseline, and we call it <em id="A8.p3.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">CMOD</em> for brevity in all experiments. We experiment with two versions of CMOD: <em id="A8.p3.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">CMOD-OMP</em> which is the originally proposed method that uses 2D-OMP as a sparse coding solver; and a variant <em id="A8.p3.1.3" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">CMOD-ADMM</em> employing an ADMM solver for the sparse coding step. While they produce similar quality results (see columns 11 and 12 in Tbl. <a href="#S6.T1" title="Table 1 ‣ 6 Experimental evaluation ‣ Sparse Coding Representation of 2-way Data" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>), the OMP version is about 3 orders of magnitude slower and required over <math id="A8.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="47" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>47</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">47</annotation></semantics></math> hours for a single run on some datasets when the target number of coding coefficients is large. As a result, in all experiments (apart from Tbl. <a href="#S6.T1" title="Table 1 ‣ 6 Experimental evaluation ‣ Sparse Coding Representation of 2-way Data" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>) we employ the CMOD-ADMM version.</p>
</div>
<div id="A8.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A8.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><em id="A8.p4.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">SeDiL</em> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib19" title="" class="ltx_ref">19</a>]</cite> is an older baseline which learns dictionaries employing a conjugate gradient approach. In our experiments, we found that it is sensitive to its hyperparameters and even when tuned extensively, it produces similar or worse results than the newer baseline CMOD <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib34" title="" class="ltx_ref">34</a>]</cite> while requiring orders of magnitude more time to complete on some datasets (see Tbl. <a href="#S6.T1" title="Table 1 ‣ 6 Experimental evaluation ‣ Sparse Coding Representation of 2-way Data" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>). Furthermore, our observations of SeDiL’s performance are consistent with those reported by the authors of CMOD <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib34" title="" class="ltx_ref">34</a>]</cite>. As a result, we report <em id="A8.p4.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">SeDiL</em> results only in Tbl. <a href="#S6.T1" title="Table 1 ‣ 6 Experimental evaluation ‣ Sparse Coding Representation of 2-way Data" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> and omit it from the comparisons in the rest of the experiments.</p>
</div>
<div id="A8.p5" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A8.p5.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A8.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Tuning.</span> We tune the hyperparameters of all competing techniques by an extensive grid search. Details of the grid search and best parameter values for all baselines are presented in Appendix <a href="#A10" title="Appendix J Hyper-parameter tuning and selection ‣ Sparse Coding Representation of 2-way Data" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">J</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<div id="A8.p6" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A8.p6.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A8.p6.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Metrics.</span> We measure the reconstruction quality as the element-wise root mean squared error between a sample <math id="A8.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{s}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{s}</annotation></semantics></math> and its reconstruction <math id="A8.p6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{s}^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>X</mi><mi>s</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{s}^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math>: <math id="A8.p6.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\text{RMSE}=\frac{1}{S}\sum_{s}^{S}\sqrt{\frac{\sum_{(i,j)}({X_{s}}_{(i,j)}-{X_{s}^{\prime}}_{(i,j)})^{2}}{|X_{s}|}}," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mtext>RMSE</mtext><mo>=</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>S</mi></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><msubsup><mo>∑</mo><mi>s</mi><mi>S</mi></msubsup><msqrt><mfrac><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="false"><msub><mo maxsize="0.700em" minsize="0.700em" stretchy="true">∑</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msub></mstyle><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mmultiscripts><mi>X</mi><mi>s</mi><mrow></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow></mrow></mmultiscripts><mo>−</mo><mmultiscripts><mi>X</mi><mi>s</mi><mo>′</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow></mrow></mmultiscripts></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mfrac></msqrt></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\text{RMSE}=\frac{1}{S}\sum_{s}^{S}\sqrt{\frac{\sum_{(i,j)}({X_{s}}_{(i,j)}-{X_{s}^{\prime}}_{(i,j)})^{2}}{|X_{s}|}},</annotation></semantics></math> where <math id="A8.p6.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|X_{s}|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|X_{s}|</annotation></semantics></math> denotes the number of elements in <math id="A8.p6.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{s}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{s}</annotation></semantics></math>.
We employ the average number of non-zero coefficients (NNZ) across samples to quantify the size of the encodings produced by competing techniques. We also measure actual running time for competing techniques to compare their scalability.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A9" class="ltx_appendix">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_appendix">Appendix I </span>Additional experiments</h2>

<figure id="A9.F5" class="ltx_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_1">
<figure id="A9.F5.sf1" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_center"><img src="2509.10033v1/syn-f1-snr-nnz.png" id="A9.F5.sf1.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:110/83;" width="110" height="83" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">(a) </span>SNR vs NNZ</figcaption>
</figure></div><div class="ltx_flex_break"></div><div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_1">
<figure id="A9.F5.sf2" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_center"><img src="2509.10033v1/syn-snr30-rmse.png" id="A9.F5.sf2.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:110/83;" width="110" height="83" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">(b) </span>NNZ vs RMSE</figcaption>
</figure></div><div class="ltx_flex_break"></div><div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_2">
<figure id="A9.F5.sf3" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_center"><img src="2509.10033v1/syn_rmse_snr2_noisy.png" id="A9.F5.sf3.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:110/83;" width="110" height="83" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">(c) </span>NNZ vs RMSE (noisy)</figcaption>
</figure></div><div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_2">
<figure id="A9.F5.sf4" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_center"><img src="2509.10033v1/syn_rmse_snr2_clean.png" id="A9.F5.sf4.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:110/83;" width="110" height="83" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">(d) </span>NNZ vs RMSE (clean)</figcaption>
</figure></div></div>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 5: </span>Evaluation on noisy synthetic data. <a href="#A9.F5.sf1" title="In Figure 5 ‣ Appendix I Additional experiments ‣ Sparse Coding Representation of 2-way Data" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5(a)</span></a>: SNR vs NNZ for settings in which CMOD and AODL recover the GT dictionaries. <a href="#A9.F5.sf2" title="In Figure 5 ‣ Appendix I Additional experiments ‣ Sparse Coding Representation of 2-way Data" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5(b)</span></a>: NNZ vs RMSE for <math id="A9.F5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="SNR=30" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>N</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>R</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>30</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">SNR=30</annotation></semantics></math>; <a href="#A9.F5.sf3" title="In Figure 5 ‣ Appendix I Additional experiments ‣ Sparse Coding Representation of 2-way Data" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5(c)</span></a> NNZ vs RMSE for SNR = 2 while representing the noisy data (clean + noise) and <a href="#A9.F5.sf4" title="In Figure 5 ‣ Appendix I Additional experiments ‣ Sparse Coding Representation of 2-way Data" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5(d)</span></a> NNZ vs RMSE for <math id="A9.F5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="SNR=2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>N</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>R</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">SNR=2</annotation></semantics></math> w.r.t. the clean data only.
</figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="A9.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A9.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A9.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Synthetic data: dictionary recovery and data reconstruction in the presence of noise.</span> Next we evaluate the ability of <span id="A9.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_markedasmath">AODL</span> (and baselines) to encode data in the presence of noise and also their ability to recover dictionaries similar to the ground truth (GT) dictionaries used to generate the data. In all synthetic tests, TGSD has the advantage of encoding with the GT dictionaries and as a result could be viewed as a GT baseline that CMOD and <span id="A9.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_markedasmath">AODL</span> can approach, but not necessarily beat.</p>
</div>
<div id="A9.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A9.p2.1" class="ltx_p">In Fig. <a href="#A9.F5.sf1" title="In Figure 5 ‣ Appendix I Additional experiments ‣ Sparse Coding Representation of 2-way Data" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5(a)</span></a> we vary the noise level quantified as SNR (signal to noise ratio) in the range <math id="A9.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[30,10,5,2]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>30</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>10</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>5</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[30,10,5,2]</annotation></semantics></math>(dB). Each sample of the ground truth signal is produced via the low-rank encoding model with a total of <math id="A9.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="30" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>30</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">30</annotation></semantics></math> NNZ coefficients and by employing random GT dictionaries. We report the NNZ of each method when for hyperparameter settings with which both CMOD and <span id="A9.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_markedasmath">AODL</span> can perfectly recover the GT dictionaries (best matching pairs of GT and learned atoms have a cosine similarity exceeding <math id="A9.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0.99" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0.99</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0.99</annotation></semantics></math>). While both CMOD and our method <span id="A9.p2.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_markedasmath">AODL</span> can both recover the GT dictionaries, <span id="A9.p2.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_markedasmath">AODL</span> produces this result with less NNZs in its encoding (with largest advantage gap in the noisiest setting SNR = 2). Both <span id="A9.p2.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_markedasmath">AODL</span> and CMOD use more coefficients than TGSD to present the data (the latter uses GT dictionaries), however, thanks to <span id="A9.p2.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_markedasmath">AODL</span>’s low rank model it requires fewer coefficients. We further visualize the alignment of learned and GT dictionaries from this experiment in Fig. <a href="#A9.F6" title="Figure 6 ‣ Appendix I Additional experiments ‣ Sparse Coding Representation of 2-way Data" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a>. The NNZ are fixed (up to 80), we plot the alignment of the learned dictionaries with the GT dictionaries. It is clear that <span id="A9.p2.1.6" class="ltx_text ltx_markedasmath">AODL</span> can almost perfectly recover the GT dictionaries. In the meantime, the dictionaries learned by CMOD is much more noisy. In general for more complex scenarios, there may be multiple sets of dictionaries that can produce low representation error and expecting a perfect reconstruction of GT dictionaries might not be feasible (or even desirable).</p>
</div>
<div id="A9.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A9.p3.1" class="ltx_p">In Fig. <a href="#A9.F5.sf2" title="In Figure 5 ‣ Appendix I Additional experiments ‣ Sparse Coding Representation of 2-way Data" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5(b)</span></a>, we plot the reconstruction error (RMSE) of all methods at different sparsity levels for SNR=30db. <span id="A9.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_markedasmath">AODL</span> is closely aligned with TGSD which uses the GT dictionaries while CMOD requires more coefficients to achieve the same RMSE levels. Figs. <a href="#A9.F5.sf3" title="In Figure 5 ‣ Appendix I Additional experiments ‣ Sparse Coding Representation of 2-way Data" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5(c)</span></a> and <a href="#A9.F5.sf4" title="In Figure 5 ‣ Appendix I Additional experiments ‣ Sparse Coding Representation of 2-way Data" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5(d)</span></a> present the RMSE v.s. NNZ trade-off in a much noisier setting (SNR = 2db). While the methods are executed on noisy data (sparse coding from GT dictionary + noise) we seek to quantify the quality of fit to the “clean” component of the samples as well as the noisy samples. Thus, we report the RMSE computed with respect to noisy data (i.e. clean + noise) in Fig. <a href="#A9.F5.sf3" title="In Figure 5 ‣ Appendix I Additional experiments ‣ Sparse Coding Representation of 2-way Data" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5(c)</span></a>; and with respect to only the clean component of the data in Fig. <a href="#A9.F5.sf4" title="In Figure 5 ‣ Appendix I Additional experiments ‣ Sparse Coding Representation of 2-way Data" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5(d)</span></a>. CMOD requires significantly more coefficients than <span id="A9.p3.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_markedasmath">AODL</span> to achieve the same RMSE levels. This is likely due to the low-rank encoding model in <span id="A9.p3.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_markedasmath">AODL</span> acting as a noise filter. Fig. <a href="#A9.F5.sf4" title="In Figure 5 ‣ Appendix I Additional experiments ‣ Sparse Coding Representation of 2-way Data" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5(d)</span></a> also suggests that CMOD likely uses coefficients to represent noise since its quality in clean data is worse than that in noisy data at high NNZs. <span id="A9.p3.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_markedasmath">AODL</span> is able to capture the clean component in the data much better, and its curve is closer to that of TGSD which employs the GT dictionaries.</p>
</div>
<figure id="A9.F6" class="ltx_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_1">
<figure id="A9.F6.sf1" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_center"><img src="2509.10033v1/D-alignment-aodl.png" id="A9.F6.sf1.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:110/85;" width="110" height="85" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">(a) </span>L alignment (AODL)</figcaption>
</figure></div><div class="ltx_flex_break"></div><div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_1">
<figure id="A9.F6.sf2" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_center"><img src="2509.10033v1/D-alignment-cmod.png" id="A9.F6.sf2.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:110/85;" width="110" height="85" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">(b) </span>L alignment (CMOD)</figcaption>
</figure></div><div class="ltx_flex_break"></div><div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_1">
<figure id="A9.F6.sf3" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_center"><img src="2509.10033v1/R-alignment-aodl.png" id="A9.F6.sf3.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:110/85;" width="110" height="85" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">(c) </span>R alignment (AODL)</figcaption>
</figure></div><div class="ltx_flex_break"></div><div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_1">
<figure id="A9.F6.sf4" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_center"><img src="2509.10033v1/R-alignment-cmod.png" id="A9.F6.sf4.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:110/85;" width="110" height="85" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">(d) </span>R alignment (CMOD)</figcaption>
</figure></div></div>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 6: </span> Alignment of the learned dictionary atoms (measured as inner products) with the ground truth dictionary atoms (when SNR = 2, NNZ for both methods are up to 80) in synthetic data. Identity matrix (1s on diagonal and 0s off-diagonal) corresponds to perfect atom recovery.
</figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="A9.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A9.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A9.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_markedasmath ltx_font_bold">AODL</span><span id="A9.p4.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> vs Random Dictionaries</span> To demonstrate the learned dictionary does help in producing better reconstruction error since low rank model by nature provide better NNZ. Instead of comparing <span id="A9.p4.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_markedasmath">AODL</span> with the ground truth dictionaries, we compare it with random generated dictionaries. In this test, the basic settings are the same with synthetic test settings. We generated two random dictionaries that serve as the left and the right dictionaries, and we use them directly with our low rank sparse coding model, which is named RAND. We can clearly see in Fig <a href="#A9.F7" title="Figure 7 ‣ Appendix I Additional experiments ‣ Sparse Coding Representation of 2-way Data" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7</span></a>, with the same level of NNZ, RAND’s reconstruction error is much higher than <span id="A9.p4.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_markedasmath">AODL</span>.</p>
</div>
<figure id="A9.F7" class="ltx_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_2">
<figure id="A9.F7.sf1" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_center"><img src="2509.10033v1/ab-test-rand.png" id="A9.F7.sf1.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:143/108;" width="143" height="108" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">(a) </span><span id="A9.F7.sf1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_markedasmath">AODL</span> v.s. Random dictionaries</figcaption>
</figure></div><div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_2">
<figure id="A9.F7.sf2" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_center"><img src="2509.10033v1/ab_twitch.png" id="A9.F7.sf2.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:143/108;" width="143" height="108" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">(b) </span>Slicing Twitch data over time</figcaption>
</figure></div></div>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 7: </span><a href="#A9.F7.sf1" title="In Figure 7 ‣ Appendix I Additional experiments ‣ Sparse Coding Representation of 2-way Data" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7(a)</span></a> Comparison of <span id="A9.F7.3" class="ltx_text ltx_markedasmath">AODL</span> and TGSD when using random dictionaries. Since low rank model is providing better NNZ, we can see that the learned dictionaries is important in representing the data. <a href="#A9.F7.sf2" title="In Figure 7 ‣ Appendix I Additional experiments ‣ Sparse Coding Representation of 2-way Data" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7(b)</span></a> In all tests, we split the data on the larger dimension. This figure shows limitations when slicing the data on the shorter dimension since the size of the encoding matrices is decided by <math id="A9.F7.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k,P,Q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>P</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>Q</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k,P,Q</annotation></semantics></math> (more details in the explanation below). </figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="A9.p5" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A9.p5.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A9.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Slicing the data on the smaller dimension</span> As mentioned in the real-world test, we slice the data into multiple samples on the longer dimension to reduce the calculation cost and also prevent learning large dictionaries. However, our goal is just dividing the data into multiple samples to fit our model and the baselines, we are free to slice on any dimension. In the previous test, we slice Twitch data who has <math id="A9.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="9000" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>9000</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">9000</annotation></semantics></math> nodes and <math id="A9.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="512" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>512</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">512</annotation></semantics></math> timesteps into <math id="A9.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="30" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>30</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">30</annotation></semantics></math> samples on the node direction. Here, we slice it on the time direction into <math id="A9.p5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="16" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>16</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">16</annotation></semantics></math> samples, each sample has a size of <math id="A9.p5.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{R}^{9000\times 32}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mn>9000</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mn>32</mn></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{R}^{9000\times 32}</annotation></semantics></math>. From Fig <a href="#A9.F7.sf2" title="In Figure 7 ‣ Appendix I Additional experiments ‣ Sparse Coding Representation of 2-way Data" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7(b)</span></a>, we can see our method <span id="A9.p5.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_markedasmath">AODL</span> still has some advantage regarding NNZ vs RMSE, however, the difference is vary limited comparing with CMOD. This is because the dictionary <math id="A9.p5.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R\in\mathbb{R}^{32\times 32}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mn>32</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mn>32</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R\in\mathbb{R}^{32\times 32}</annotation></semantics></math> has a very small size (atom number), and it is close to the rank parameter <math id="A9.p5.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> we choose. We know that the number of coefficients of <span id="A9.p5.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_markedasmath">AODL</span> is NNZ(Y) + NNZ(W), while for CMOD, the number of coefficients is NNZ(Z). The size of Y is <math id="A9.p5.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P\times k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P\times k</annotation></semantics></math>; and size of W is <math id="A9.p5.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k\times Q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>Q</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k\times Q</annotation></semantics></math>. So, the total size of <span id="A9.p5.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_markedasmath">AODL</span> would be <math id="A9.p5.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Pk+kQ=k(P+Q)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>Q</mi></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>Q</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Pk+kQ=k(P+Q)</annotation></semantics></math>. While the size of <math id="A9.p5.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Z</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z</annotation></semantics></math> in CMOD is just <math id="A9.p5.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P\times Q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>Q</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P\times Q</annotation></semantics></math>. The advantage of our method <span id="A9.p5.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_markedasmath">AODL</span> will be large when <math id="A9.p5.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k(P+Q)&lt;&lt;PQ" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>Q</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>&lt;&lt;</mo><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>Q</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k(P+Q)&lt;&lt;PQ</annotation></semantics></math>. Since we always prefer smaller <math id="A9.p5.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>, if either <math id="A9.p5.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>P</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P</annotation></semantics></math> or <math id="A9.p5.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q</annotation></semantics></math> is small as well, our advantage will be vanished. As a result, our model will always prefer the two learned dictionaries to have relatively large size, and a small rank <math id="A9.p5.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
