<td id="A9.T2.2.6.9" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_align_top ltx_border_b ltx_border_r ltx_border_t" style="padding-left:3.0pt;padding-right:3.0pt;">
<span id="A9.T2.2.6.9.1" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_align_top" style="width:20.7pt;">
<span id="A9.T2.2.6.9.1.1" class="ltx_p">Vary</span>
</span></td>
<td id="A9.T2.2.6.10" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_align_top ltx_border_b ltx_border_r ltx_border_t" style="padding-left:3.0pt;padding-right:3.0pt;">
<span id="A9.T2.2.6.10.1" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_align_top" style="width:20.7pt;">
<span id="A9.T2.2.6.10.1.1" class="ltx_p">Vary</span>
</span></td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table">Table 2: </span>Parameters for competing methods where <math id="A9.T2.m56" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda_{1},\lambda_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>λ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>λ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda_{1},\lambda_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> are sparsity parameters for ADMM sparse solver; <math id="A9.T2.m57" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>T</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> is the targeting number of coefficients for 2D-OMP; <math id="A9.T2.m58" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> is the rank parameter of TGSD. Some methods are not included in the theoretical test the corresponding cells are marked as NA for Not Applicable. Ranges for tested values are listed in the Range column.
</figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
<section id="A10" class="ltx_appendix">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_appendix">Appendix J </span>Hyper-parameter tuning and selection</h2>

<div id="A10.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A10.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The parameter settings for all competing techniques unless otherwise specified are as follows. For low rank models like TGSD and <span id="A10.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_markedasmath">AODL</span>, we set <math id="A10.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k=3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k=3</annotation></semantics></math> in synthetic test, and <math id="A10.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> is varying from <math id="A10.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[15,30,45]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>15</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>30</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>45</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[15,30,45]</annotation></semantics></math> in real-world reconstruction test and missing value imputations tests. In the case study, the dictionaries is calculated at <math id="A10.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k=15" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>15</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k=15</annotation></semantics></math>. In addition to grid search for <math id="A10.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>, as mentioned in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib27" title="" class="ltx_ref">27</a>]</cite>, one could estimate the rank <math id="A10.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> in the same way as in PCA or SVD.
The <math id="A10.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>λ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda</annotation></semantics></math>s are sparsity parameter for ADMM method. In CMOD, we only have <math id="A10.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>λ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> since only one encoding matrix is calculated, and in TGSD, we have <math id="A10.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>λ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A10.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>λ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda_{2}</annotation></semantics></math>. We grid search these parameter in range <math id="A10.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[10^{-3},10^{-2},\cdots,10^{3}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><msup><mn>10</mn><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mn>10</mn><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo>,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mo>,</mo><msup><mn>10</mn><mn>3</mn></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[10^{-3},10^{-2},\cdots,10^{3}]</annotation></semantics></math> to produce curve with different NNZ and RMSE values. In CMOD-OMP, <math id="A10.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>T</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> is the only parameter, which indicates the target number of coefficients in the encoding matrix. OMP models works much slower as <math id="A10.p1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>T</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> increases, as a result, we choose some relatively small targets in tests and only report them in Tbl. <a href="#S6.T1" title="Table 1 ‣ 6 Experimental evaluation ‣ Sparse Coding Representation of 2-way Data" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<div id="A10.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A10.p2.1" class="ltx_p">SeDiL is another baseline model that requires intensive parameter search. <math id="A10.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q</annotation></semantics></math> means the weight of mixed sparsity measure, which indicates how the sparsity term is being adjusted using power of <math id="A10.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q</annotation></semantics></math>. <math id="A10.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>μ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu</annotation></semantics></math> is the multiplier of the sparse matrix. <math id="A10.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>λ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda</annotation></semantics></math> is the Lagrange multiplier of the sparsity term. <math id="A10.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>β</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta</annotation></semantics></math> is the step size when updating the sparse matrix. <math id="A10.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="iter" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">iter</annotation></semantics></math> is the maximum iteration of the model. We can see that all the above parameters are affecting the performance of the sparsity of the model. We picked the values that can help us to reach the target NNZ, which is defined in Tbl. <a href="#S6.T1" title="Table 1 ‣ 6 Experimental evaluation ‣ Sparse Coding Representation of 2-way Data" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>, for a fair comparison of all models.</p>
</div>
<div id="A10.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A10.p3.1" class="ltx_p">All hyperparameter ranges are listed in Tbl. <a href="#A9.T2" title="Table 2 ‣ Appendix I Additional experiments ‣ Sparse Coding Representation of 2-way Data" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>.</p>
</div>
</section>
</article>
